2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] To use the same JSON‑RPC pattern for the `echocoms` tool, you have two clean options:
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] ╭────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] │  Option                                                                                                │  Approach                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   │  Complexity                                                                                      │
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] │  **1. Embed a lightweight HTTP server in `echocoms.exe`** (like `fit3`’s `http.h`)                     │  The  Windows utility becomes a tiny daemon that listens on a local port, accepts JSON‑RPC POST requests, and replies with COM‑port enumeration, open/write/read/close.                                                                                     │  Medium – need Winsock + basic HTTP parsing, but the existing `enumports.cpp` code is reusable.  │
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] │  **2. Extend the existing `fit3` server (or a new Linux host) to handle COM‑port operations via RPC**  │  If you want cross‑platform control of Pico devices, add `sys.ports`, `sys.open`, `sys.write`, `sys.read` methods to `headless.h`. On Windows you’d run a small stub that forwards requests to the Win32 API, or port `enumports.cpp` to Linux via `udev`.  │  Higher – requires platform abstraction and possibly a separate process per OS.                  │
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] ╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] Recommended first step – Option 1 (self‑contained Windows RPC server)
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] You already have the core pieces:
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] - `enumports.cpp` – enumerates COM ports (SetupAPI), opens, writes, reads with timeouts.
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] - `echocoms.cpp` – launches per‑port reader threads.
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] Just wrap them with a simple HTTP server (like `http.h` but simpler – only `POST /rpc` needed).
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] The RPC method set would be:
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] // Request
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] {"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"ports.list","params":[],"id":1}
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] // Response
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] {"jsonrpc":"2.0","result":[{"name":"COM5","path":"USB\\VID_2E8...","description":"Pico CDC"}],"id":1}
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] // Open port
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] {"method":"port.open","params":{"name":"COM5","baud":115200}}
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] // Write
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] {"method":"port.write","params":{"handle":1,"data":"\nBOOT\n"}}
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] // Read
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] {"method":"port.read","params":{"handle":1,"timeout":100}}
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] // Close
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] {"method":"port.close","params":{"handle":1}}
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] You can then call these from your browser’s `sendRPC()` function exactly as you do for `dsptool`.
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] This keeps the protocol uniform.
2026-06-05 13:00:56 [deepseek-v4] 
