2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] Understood – the first command over serial JSON‑RPC should set the device’s clock (RTC). This is a
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] natural bootstrap step so that logs, timestamps, and sync are correct from the start.
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] Proposed RPC method
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] • Request* (host → device):
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] {"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"setclock","params":{"timestamp":1717699200},"id":1}
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] The `timestamp` is Unix epoch seconds (or milliseconds, depending on your precision needs). The
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] device should set its internal RTC and respond with something like:
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] • Response* (device → host):
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] {"jsonrpc":"2.0","result":"OK","id":1}
2026-06-05 13:02:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] Host‑side implementation (in `echocoms.cpp`)
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] You already have the building blocks in `enumports.cpp` (open, write, read). You need:
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 1. A function to build the JSON‑RPC string.
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 2. A function to send and receive one line over the serial port.
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 3. Call it right after opening the port.
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] For example:
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] RpcCall(h, "setclock", "{\"timestamp\":" + std::to_string(time(nullptr)) + "}", reply, 500);
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] The `RpcCall` function would:
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] - Construct:
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] `"{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"setclock\",\"params\":{\"timestamp\":...},\"id\":1}\n"`
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] - Send using `WriteFile`.
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] - Read lines using `ReadFile` with timeouts until a line containing `"result"` is found, or until
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] timeout.
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] The existing `readComPort` (which sets `ReadIntervalTimeout`) can be used in a loop to accumulate
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] characters until `\n`.
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] On the Pico side
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] The firmware (likely in TinyUSB CDC) should:
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] - Buffer incoming characters until `\n`.
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] - Parse the JSON, execute `setclock`.
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] - Send back the response followed by `\n`.
2026-06-05 13:02:26 [deepseek-v4] 
