2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] The “internet time protocol” pattern typically involves a 48‑byte packet with 64‑bit timestamps
2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] (originate, receive, transmit). Over serial you could simplify to:
2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] [0x01]  [command]  [8 byte timestamp]  [checksum]
2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] Where command `0x01` means “set clock”, and the Pico responds with `0x02` + its idea of current
2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] time.
2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:43:27 [deepseek-v4] If you want to keep the firmware simpler (no JSON parser), this is attractive. If you already have
