2026-06-05 13:44:20 [nitro@ryzen5] what is internet time protocol called?
2026-06-05 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.047 822.8KB 4.49s
2026-06-05 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4] The protocol you're thinking of is called NTP (Network Time Protocol). A simpler version
2026-06-05 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4] used in many embedded devices is SNTP (Simple Network Time Protocol), which is a subset of
2026-06-05 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4] NTP.
2026-06-05 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4] Both use a compact binary packet (typically 48 or 64 bytes) with 64‑bit timestamps and a round‑trip
2026-06-05 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4] measurement mechanism. That's the “internet time protocol” pattern you described.
2026-06-05 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4] 
