2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.048 823.4KB 8.59s
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] Yes, using JSON‑RPC to carry SNTP‑style timestamp exchange is perfectly fine for your use case. The
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] overhead is modest on a 115200 baud serial link (a JSON frame might be ~100 bytes, which takes
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] about 1 ms to transmit – negligible compared to the Pico’s clock drift).
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] Just embed the SNTP fields (originate, receive, transmit timestamps) as JSON numbers or strings in
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] the parameters. The host sends:
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] {"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"sntp","params":{"originate":<hostTime>},"id":1}
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] The Pico replies with:
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] {"jsonrpc":"2.0","result":{"receive":<picoReceiveTime>,"transmit":<picoTransmitTime>},"id":1}
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] The host then computes round‑trip delay and offset to adjust its own clock or to log the Pico’s
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] time accuracy.
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] This keeps your RPC system uniform – no separate binary protocol needed. If later you need
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] sub‑millisecond precision you could switch to a binary packet, but for a firmware clock sync over
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] serial this is more than adequate.
2026-06-05 13:44:57 [deepseek-v4] 
