2026-06-05 16:23:00 [nitro@ryzen5] any conventions for getters and setters with json rpc?
2026-06-05 16:23:08 [deepseek-v4]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.048 827.1KB 7.45s
2026-06-05 16:23:08 [deepseek-v4] There is no formal standard for getter/setter naming in JSON‑RPC, but common conventions
2026-06-05 16:23:08 [deepseek-v4] have emerged:
2026-06-05 16:23:08 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 16:23:08 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 16:23:08 [deepseek-v4] ╭──────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-06-05 16:23:08 [deepseek-v4] │  Pattern                 │  Example                                                                                       │
2026-06-05 16:23:08 [deepseek-v4] ├──────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] │  **Verb‑noun**           │  `getClock`, `setClock`, `getTemperature`, `setGain`                                           │
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] │  **Noun‑verb**           │  `clock.get`, `clock.set`, `temperature.get`, `gain.set`                                       │
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] │  **Combined state**      │  `clock` with an optional `value` parameter – omitting it acts as a getter, providing it as a  │
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] │                          │  setter. Return the current state. This is RPC‑style idempotent if you treat it as a read/write │
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] │                          │  property.                                                                                     │
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] │  **CRUD‑like**           │  `readClock`, `writeClock` (less common for RPC, more for REST)                                │
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] ╰──────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] The verb‑noun (`setClock`) is the most widely used in JSON‑RPC APIs because each method name is
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] self‑explaining and maps directly to a procedure call. The combined state pattern is elegant when
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] you want a single method for both reading and writing (e.g., `clock` with an optional
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] `params.timestamp`).
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] Pick one and be consistent across your entire API. For your Pico, `setClock` and `getClock` (or the
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] combined `clock`) are fine choices.
2026-06-05 16:23:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 16:24:08 [nitro@ryzen5] so setClock could have unix seconds epoch seconds
2026-06-05 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.048 829.2KB 5.08s
2026-06-05 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4] Yes, using Unix epoch seconds (or milliseconds) as the parameter for `setClock` is a natural choice
2026-06-05 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4] – it's universally recognised and easy to handle on both the host and the Pico.
2026-06-05 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4] If you want sub‑second precision, milliseconds (13‑digit int) work well; otherwise seconds
2026-06-05 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4] (10‑digit) suffice for most cases. The Pico’s `rtc_set_datetime` expects a `datetime_t` struct, so
2026-06-05 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4] you’ll convert the epoch to year/month/day/hour/minute/second on the device side. That’s
2026-06-05 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4] straightforward using standard C time functions (gmtime_r etc.).
2026-06-05 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4] 
