2026-06-05 17:24:34 [nitro@ryzen5] why incomplete type
2026-06-05 17:24:35 [nitro@ryzen5] struct JSObject{	std::vector<utf8> names;	std::vector<JSValue*> values;	virtual ~JSObject(){	}
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 585B 9.72s
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] The error usually occurs because `utf8` is an incomplete type at the point where
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] `std::vector<utf8>` is instantiated. In C++, `std::vector<T>` requires `T` to be a complete
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] type (its full definition must be visible) because the vector stores objects by value—it needs
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] to know the size and layout of `T` to allocate memory, call constructors/destructors, etc.
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] In your code:
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] struct JSObject {
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4]     std::vector<utf8> names;   // <-- utf8 must be complete
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4]     std::vector<JSValue*> values; // <-- JSValue* is fine even if JSValue is incomplete
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4]     virtual ~JSObject() {}
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] };
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] - `std::vector<JSValue*>` only needs the size of a pointer, which is known even if `JSValue` is
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] only forward‑declared.
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] - `std::vector<utf8>` needs the full definition of `utf8`. If `utf8` is merely declared (e.g.,
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] `class utf8;`) or not defined at all, you get an incomplete type error.
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] • Common fixes*:
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 1. Include the full definition of `utf8` (or `JSValue` if it appears as a value type
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] elsewhere).
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4]    For example, if `utf8` is an alias for `std::string`, make sure you have `#include <string>` and
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] `using utf8 = std::string;` before the struct definition.
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 2. Forward‑declare correctly if `utf8` is a class defined in another header. You cannot use
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] it in a `std::vector` with only a forward declaration.
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 3. Check for missing includes – if `utf8` is defined in your own project, ensure its header
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] is included before `JSObject`.
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] If you intended `utf8` to be a primitive type (like `char8_t*`), then the vector should be
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] `std::vector<char8_t>` or similar, but the name `utf8` suggests a custom string type.
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] Please share the definition (or declaration) of `utf8` and any relevant includes, and I can give
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] more specific advice.
2026-06-05 17:24:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:25:00 [nitro@ryzen5] typedef std::string utf8;
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 2.561KB 5.66s
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] The fact that `utf8` is a `typedef` for `std::string` is key. The "incomplete type" error you saw
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] almost certainly means that the full definition of `std::string` (or the `typedef` itself) was
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] not visible at the point where `std::vector<utf8>` was instantiated.
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] In C++, `std::vector<T>` requires `T` to be a complete type—the compiler must know its
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] size, default constructor, destructor, etc. Since `std::string` needs its own includes
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] (`<string>`), the most common cause is:
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 1. Missing `#include <string>` before the struct definition.
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 2. The `typedef std::string utf8;` appears after the struct, or inside a different
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] namespace / scope than where the vector is declared.
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 3. The typedef might be conditional or hidden behind a preprocessor guard.
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] • To fix it, place the following before* `struct JSObject`:
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] #include <string>
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] typedef std::string utf8;
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] Then your struct compiles correctly because `std::string` is now a complete type.
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] If you already have both, double‑check that there’s no circular include, and that the `typedef` is
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] not inside a forward‑declared class or forgotten namespace. The error message should point exactly
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] to the line where `std::vector<utf8>` is declared—that line is where the compiler noticed the
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] incomplete type.
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] If you still have issues, please share the exact compiler error text and the order of
2026-06-05 17:25:07 [deepseek-v4] includes/definitions in your file. I’m happy to help further.
