2026-06-05 17:27:21 [nitro@ryzen5] is there std mutex in pico rp2350?
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 4.229KB 10.75s
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] No, `std::mutex` is not available by default on the Raspberry Pi Pico RP2350 when using the
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] standard Pico SDK and toolchain (e.g., `arm-none-eabi-g++` with Newlib‑nano or picolibc).
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] Why?
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] - `std::mutex` depends on threading primitives provided by an operating system (e.g.,
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] `pthread_mutex` on Linux) or a C++ runtime that supports `std::thread`.
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] - The bare‑metal Pico SDK does not include a threading implementation — it runs directly on
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] hardware.
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] - The C++ standard library (newlib‑nano / picolibc) shipped with the GNU ARM embedded toolchain
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] does not implement `std::mutex` correctly in this environment. The compiler will either
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] reject it (compile‑time error) or emit a linker error for missing symbols.
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] What you can use instead
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] The Pico SDK provides efficient, hardware‑supported synchronization primitives for the RP2350’s
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] dual‑core architecture:
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] ╭─────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] │  Primitive              │  Description                                       │
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] ├─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] │  `mutex_t`              │  Blocking mutex (uses SDK scheduler if available)  │
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] │  `critical_section_t`   │  Disables interrupts (spinlock)                    │
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] │  `spin_lock_t`          │  Hardware spinlock (for low‑level synchronization) │
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] │  `sync.h` functions     │  `__sync_*` builtins, atomic operations            │
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] ╰─────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] Example using `mutex_t`:
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] #include "pico/mutex.h"
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] mutex_t my_mutex;
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] mutex_init(&my_mutex);
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] mutex_enter_blocking(&my_mutex);
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] // critical section
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] mutex_exit(&my_mutex);
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] What about FreeRTOS / other RTOS?
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] If you add an RTOS like FreeRTOS (e.g., using `pico-enable-freertos` or manual integration),
2026-06-05 17:27:32 [deepseek-v4] `std::mutex` may become available — but that requires:
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] - The RTOS must provide a `pthread_mutex` or equivalent adaptation layer.
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] - The C++ library must be configured with `_POSIX_THREADS` support (often not the default with
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] newlib‑nano).
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] - In practice, most Pico + FreeRTOS projects still use the SDK's `mutex_t` or FreeRTOS’s own
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] `xSemaphoreCreateMutex` rather than `std::mutex`, because the C++ library support is incomplete.
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] Summary
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] - Bare‑metal: No `std::mutex`. Use SDK primitives (`mutex_t`, `critical_section`,
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] `spin_lock`).
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] - With FreeRTOS: Possibly, but not guaranteed; RTOS‑specific mutexes are safer and simpler.
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] If you need C++ standard threading, consider a different platform (e.g., Linux, ESP32 with ESP‑IDF)
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] where `std::mutex` works out of the box.
2026-06-05 17:27:33 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 18:18:28 [nitro@ryzen5] does pico sdk have a performance counter
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 504B 7.24s
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] Yes, the Raspberry Pi Pico SDK includes support for performance counters, primarily through the
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] Data Watchpoint and Trace (DWT) unit's cycle counter on the RP2040 microcontroller.
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] The SDK provides a dedicated library called `pico_perf_counter` which wraps the DWT cycle
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] counter and offers functions for measuring CPU cycles with high accuracy. You can enable it by
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] adding `pico_perf_counter` to your CMake target's `target_link_libraries`. Basic usage:
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] - `perf_counter_init()` — initializes the DWT counter.
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] - `perf_counter_begin()` / `perf_counter_end()` — marks the start/end of a measurement interval.
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] - `perf_counter_get_count()` — returns the elapsed cycles since initialization.
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] - `perf_counter_get_elapsed()` — returns cycles between the last begin and end calls.
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] Alternatively, you can directly access the DWT cycle counter via `hardware_dwt`. The relevant
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] functions (available when `<hardware/dwt.h>` is included) include:
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] - `dwt_init()` — enables the cycle counter.
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] - `dwt_get_cycle_count()` — reads the current cycle count.
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] - `dwt_reset_cycle_count()` — resets the counter to zero.
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] Both approaches provide cycle‑accurate timing for performance analysis, benchmarking, and
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] profiling.
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] If you need a higher‑resolution counter beyond the 48 MHz system clock (e.g., for sub‑cycle
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] measurements), that is not available on the RP2040, but the DWT cycle counter is sufficient for
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] most performance‑sensitive code.
2026-06-05 18:18:36 [deepseek-v4] 
