2026-06-13 08:41:38 [nitro@ryzen5] pinout for vga connector
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.004 716B 23.88s
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] Here is the standard DE-15 VGA connector pinout (15-pin D-sub, HD-15):
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] Pin layout (looking at the mating face of the connector)
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code]   1   2   3   4   5
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code]    6   7   8   9  10
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code]     11  12  13  14  15
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] Pin assignments
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] ╭─────────┬─────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Pin    │  Signal         │  Description                               │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] ├─────────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  1      │  **RED**        │  Red video                                 │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  2      │  **GREEN**      │  Green video                               │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  3      │  **BLUE**       │  Blue video                                │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  4      │  **ID2 / RES**  │  Monitor ID bit 2 (often unused/reserved)  │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  5      │  **GND**        │  Ground (H-sync return)                    │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  6      │  **RED RTN**    │  Red return/ground                         │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  7      │  **GREEN RTN**  │  Green return/ground                       │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  8      │  **BLUE RTN**   │  Blue return/ground                        │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  9      │  **+5 V**       │  +5 V DC (fused, DDC supply)               │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  10     │  **GND**        │  Ground (sync return)                      │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  11     │  **ID0 / RES**  │  Monitor ID bit 0 (often unused/reserved)  │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  12     │  **SDA**        │  DDC/EDID I²C data                         │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  13     │  **H-SYNC**     │  Horizontal sync                           │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  14     │  **V-SYNC**     │  Vertical sync                             │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  15     │  **SCL**        │  DDC/EDID I²C clock                        │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Shell  │  **Shield**     │  Chassis/shield ground                     │
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] ╰─────────┴─────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] Key notes
2026-06-13 08:42:03 [kimi-k2.7-code] - RGB lines are 75 Ω impedance coaxial signals.
2026-06-13 08:42:04 [kimi-k2.7-code] - Sync: H-sync and V-sync are TTL-level signals (5 V).
2026-06-13 08:42:04 [kimi-k2.7-code] - DDC (pins 9, 12, 15) allows the monitor to send EDID information to the source.
2026-06-13 08:42:04 [kimi-k2.7-code] -
2026-06-13 08:42:04 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:33 [nitro@ryzen5] adding vga output to microcontroller can i start with just i2c 3 wire?
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.005 2.974KB 21.24s
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] You can start simple, but I need to clarify: I²C is not used for VGA video output. The I²C
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] pins on a VGA connector (pins 9, 12, 15) are only for DDC/EDID — they let the monitor tell
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] the source its capabilities. The actual video uses analog RGB + sync.
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] So if you want VGA video output from a microcontroller, the absolute minimum is:
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] Minimum working VGA: 3 signal wires + ground
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] You can start with just one color, plus the two sync signals:
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] ╭───────────────────────┬──────────────┬───────────────────────────────╮
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Signal               │  Pin         │  MCU connection               │
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] ├───────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Green (or Red/Blue)  │  2 (or 1/3)  │  DAC / PWM / resistor ladder  │
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  H-sync               │  13          │  GPIO timer output            │
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  V-sync               │  14          │  GPIO timer output            │
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Ground               │  5, 6–8, 10  │  MCU GND                      │
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] ╰───────────────────────┴──────────────┴───────────────────────────────╯
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] This gives you monochrome output. Green is usually best because many monitors use it for
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] sync-on-green detection and it tends to look cleanest.
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:55 [kimi-k2.7-code] If you want color
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] You need three analog outputs for R, G, B, plus H-sync and V-sync:
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] ╭──────────┬──────────────┬─────────────────╮
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Signal  │  Pin         │  Notes          │
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] ├──────────┼──────────────┼─────────────────┤
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Red     │  1           │  0–0.7 V, 75 Ω  │
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Green   │  2           │  0–0.7 V, 75 Ω  │
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Blue    │  3           │  0–0.7 V, 75 Ω  │
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  H-sync  │  13          │  5 V TTL        │
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  V-sync  │  14          │  5 V TTL        │
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  GND     │  5, 6–8, 10  │                 │
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] ╰──────────┴──────────────┴─────────────────╯
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] What you actually need from the MCU
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] - Two sync pins that can generate precise timing (often a timer with PWM or direct GPIO
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] bit-banging).
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] - One or three DAC channels for the video signal. You can build these with resistor ladders
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] (R-2R) if your MCU lacks a DAC.
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] So to answer your question
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] - If you mean 3 wires total (one color + H-sync + V-sync): yes, that works for
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] monochrome VGA.
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] - If you mean I²C only: no, a VGA monitor will not display video from I²C.
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] What microcontroller are you using? That determines whether you can use a hardware DAC, SPI
2026-06-13 08:43:56 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:13 [nitro@ryzen5] i said to start, or is there a resoloution that all VGA monitors will support?
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.006 5.634KB 19.78s
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] Yes — 640 × 480 at 60 Hz is the baseline resolution that every VGA monitor must support. If
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] you output those timings, basically any VGA display will sync to it.
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] Minimum wiring to start
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] You can absolutely start with just 3 signal wires plus ground. No I²C/DDC is required.
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] Leave pins 9, 12, and 15 unconnected — the monitor will just default to 640 × 480.
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] ╭───────────────────────┬──────────────┬───────────────────────────────────╮
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Wire                 │  VGA pin     │  Purpose                          │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] ├───────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  H-sync               │  13          │  Tells monitor end of each line   │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  V-sync               │  14          │  Tells monitor end of each frame  │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Green (or Red/Blue)  │  2           │  Video intensity                  │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Ground               │  5, 6–8, 10  │  Common return                    │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] ╰───────────────────────┴──────────────┴───────────────────────────────────╯
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] That gives you monochrome output. Tie the unused R and B lines to ground through 75 Ω resistors, or
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] just leave them grounded.
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 640 × 480 @ 60 Hz timings
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] This is the safest “universal VGA” mode.
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] ╭──────────────────────────┬──────────────────╮
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Parameter               │  Value           │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] ├──────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Pixel clock             │  25.175 MHz      │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Horizontal active       │  640 pixels      │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Horizontal front porch  │  16 pixels       │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Horizontal sync         │  96 pixels       │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Horizontal back porch   │  48 pixels       │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  **Horizontal total**    │  **800 pixels**  │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Vertical active         │  480 lines       │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Vertical front porch    │  10 lines        │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Vertical sync           │  2 lines         │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Vertical back porch     │  33 lines        │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  **Vertical total**      │  **525 lines**   │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  Refresh rate            │  ~60 Hz          │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  H-sync polarity         │  Negative        │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] │  V-sync polarity         │  Negative        │
2026-06-13 08:45:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] ╰──────────────────────────┴──────────────────╯
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] What the monitor needs to see
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] During active video, the color line sits between 0 V (black) and about 0.7 V (full brightness) into
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] 75 Ω. During blanking (porches and sync), keep it at 0 V.
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] The sync pulses are 5 V TTL, active low:
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] - H-sync: one 3.8 µs pulse at the end of every 31.8 µs line.
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] - V-sync: two 64 µs pulses at the end of every 16.7 ms frame.
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] Microcontroller reality check
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] - 25.175 MHz pixel
2026-06-13 08:45:35 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:50:01 [nitro@ryzen5] how many PIO blocks on the RP2350?
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.003 8.390KB 6.40s
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] The RP2350 has 2 PIO blocks, the same as the RP2040.
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] Each PIO block has 4 state machines, giving you 8 state machines total.
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] Each block also has:
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] - 32 instruction slots
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] - 4 × 32-bit shift registers
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] - TX/RX FIFOs per state machine
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] - Flexible GPIO mapping
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] The RP2350 PIO is backwards-compatible with the RP2040 but adds a few new instructions and
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] features. For your VGA project, this is plenty — you could dedicate one or two PIO state machines
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] to generate the pixel clock, H-sync, and V-sync, while the CPU or DMA feeds pixel data into the
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] FIFO.
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] Do you want a sketch of how to use PIO for VGA timing on the RP2350?
2026-06-13 08:50:09 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:26 [nitro@ryzen5] with 3.3v gpio i want to generate 700mv peak to peak ac wave
2026-06-13 09:40:53 [kimi-k2.7-code]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.004 527B 25.83s
2026-06-13 09:40:53 [kimi-k2.7-code] You can do this with a resistor divider plus an AC-coupling capacitor. GPIO pins output 0–3.3 V DC,
2026-06-13 09:40:53 [kimi-k2.7-code] so you attenuate first, then block the DC offset to get a ±350 mV AC signal.
2026-06-13 09:40:53 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:53 [kimi-k2.7-code] • Voltage divider*
2026-06-13 09:40:53 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:53 [kimi-k2.7-code] Target: 700 mV p-p from 3.3 V p-p
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] \[
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] \frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}} = \frac{0.7}{3.3} \approx 0.212
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] \]
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] Use:
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] - R1 = 10 kΩ
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] - R2 = 2.7 kΩ
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] \[
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] V_{out} = 3.3 \times \frac{2.7k}{10k + 2.7k} \approx 0.70 \text{ V p-p}
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] \]
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] • AC coupling*
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] Add a capacitor in series after the divider to remove the DC offset:
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] - C = 1 µF to 10 µF for audio/low-frequency work
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] - 100 nF works fine for kHz-range signals
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] The output will swing ±350 mV around 0 V.
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] • Circuit*
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] GPIO ----[10k]----+----[2.7k]---- GND
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code]                   |
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code]                   +----[C]---- Vout (AC, 700mV p-p)
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] • If you want a sine or arbitrary waveform*
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] Use a PWM output with a low-pass filter before the divider. A simple RC filter works:
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] - R = 1 kΩ to 10 kΩ
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] - C = 1 nF to 100 nF depending on frequency
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] Then feed that filtered 0–3.3 V signal into the same divider/coupling cap.
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] • Load note*
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] The output impedance of this divider is roughly R1 || R2 ≈ 2.1 kΩ. If the next stage has low input
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] impedance, buffer it with an op-amp follower.
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] What waveform and frequency are you aiming for? Sine, square, or arbitrary? That changes the filter
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] and capacitor values.
2026-06-13 09:40:54 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
