2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Overall Architecture
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `udev.h` wraps libudev to discover USB, TTY, and input devices, storing
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] information in a `deviceInfo` structure and categorising it under `Devices`. The code is
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] Linux‑specific and relies on kernel headers (`linux/input.h`, `linux/joystick.h`).
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `json.h` provides a hand‑written JSON parser (`JSONParser`) and value representation
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] (`JSValue`, `JSObject`, `JSArray`). It also includes helper utilities (`jsonifyInts`,
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] `jsonifyStrings`, etc.).
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3. Potential Issues & Suggestions
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] Memory Management
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `deviceInfo` objects are allocated with `new` but deletion is inconsistent:
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash]   - `enumInputs` and `enumUSB` insert into `inputList` / `usbList` (a `std::set<deviceInfo*>`). The
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] set holds raw pointers, but there is no destructor in `Devices` to free them. This will cause
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] memory leaks.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash]   - In `enumTTY`, each `deviceInfo` is deleted after printing – that’s fine, but mixing ownership
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] patterns is risky.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash]   - Recommendation: Use `std::unique_ptr<deviceInfo>` or `std::shared_ptr` (or store
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] `deviceInfo` objects directly in a container). If performance is a concern, consider
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] `std::vector<deviceInfo>` and avoid allocations.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `JSValue` / JS containers are manually allocated and deleted; the destructor handles
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] `object` and `array`, but if any `JSValue` is not owned by a JSObject/JSArray (e.g., returned from
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] `JSONParser::parseJSON` directly), ownership must be managed carefully. In practice, the parser’s
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] recursive structure seems to hand over ownership to the top‑level `*result`, but this is fragile.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] JSON Parser (`json.h`)
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Standard conformance: The parser handles simple cases but may break on:
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash]   - Unicode escape sequences (`\uXXXX`) – the code has a `TODO` comment and does not parse them.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash]   - Surrogate pairs (characters above U+FFFF).
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash]   - Numbers like `-0`, `1e5` – the `find('.')` logic is simplistic.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash]   - Whitespace inside strings is correctly ignored; however, the `parseString` function does not
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] handle `\u` escapes either.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Deprecated API: `std::wstring_convert<std::codecvt_utf8<wchar_t>>` is deprecated in C++17
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] and removed in C++20. Consider using `std::wstring_convert` with `std::codecvt_utf8<char32_t>` or,
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] better yet, a library like simdjson or nlohmann/json if you can add dependencies.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The `convert32` object is used to convert `utf8` to `utf32`. This introduces a dependency
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] on `wchar_t` (platform‑dependent size) and `codecvt`. For a more portable approach, you could parse
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] UTF‑8 directly or use a small UTF‑8 decoder.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Case sensitivity: The parser identifies literals `true`, `false`, `null`; it correctly
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] avoids matching inside strings but uses `literal == "true"` etc. This works because the literal is
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] built from non‑whitespace characters outside quotes.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] General C++ Practices
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Headers: The file `nitrohost.h` is referenced but not provided. I assume it defines
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] `utf8`, `Attributes`, `Strings`, `quoted()`, `escape()`, `joinStrings()`, `nameValue()`,
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] `ErrorResult` etc. Make sure those are consistent.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `stdString` returns a reference to a global `emptyString`. While safe (the global lives
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] for the program’s duration), it prevents returning an empty string from a `const char*` that is
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] null. Could instead return a `std::string` by value (small string optimisation makes this cheap).
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Fixed‑size array `bits[32]` in `channel`: if you ever need more than 32 channels or bits,
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] consider `std::vector<int>` or `std::bitset`.
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Debug macros: `#define KB_STUFF` and `#ifdef BORK` sections should be removed or guarded
2026-06-19 16:26:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] by a dedicated feature flag.
