2026-06-19 23:57:15 [nitro@ryzen5] in bat files can you use \n type strings
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.9.0 ⛲  grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.001 508B 5.55s
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] No, batch files (`.bat` / `.cmd`) do not support `\n` escape sequences like most
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] programming languages.
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] If you write this:
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] echo Hello\nWorld
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] It will literally print `Hello\nWorld`, not a newline.
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] How to actually insert newlines in batch files
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] Here are the common ways to handle newlines:
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:21 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Multiple `echo` commands (simplest)
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] @echo off
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] echo Hello
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] echo World
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Blank line using `echo.`
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] @echo off
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] echo Hello
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] echo.
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] echo World
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Embedding a newline in a variable (most useful trick)
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] This is the standard way to store a newline character:
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] @echo off
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] setlocal enabledelayedexpansion
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] :: Create a variable containing a newline
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] set "NL=^
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] "
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] :: ↑ There must be a blank line after the caret
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] echo Line 1!NL!Line 2!NL!Line 3
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Using a function to make it cleaner
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] @echo off
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] setlocal enabledelayedexpansion
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] set "NL=^
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] "
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] call :PrintLines "First line" "Second line" "Third line"
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] exit /b
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] :PrintLines
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] for %%A in (%*) do (
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1]     echo %%~A!NL!
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] )
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] exit /b
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] Quick Tip:
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] If you need more advanced string handling (including proper newlines, escaping, etc.), it's often
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] easier to use PowerShell from your batch file:
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] powershell -Command "Write-Host 'Hello`nWorld'"
2026-06-19 23:57:22 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:23 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:57:23 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like me to show you the best approach for your specific use case? (e.g., writing to a
2026-06-19 23:57:23 [grok-build-0.1] file, using in a variable, etc.)
