2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] Here's the clean way to add a newline (`\n`) to the end of your string in C++:
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] Basic Solution
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] std::string payload = argv[1];
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] payload += '\n';
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] More Complete & Safer Version
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] You should also check that an argument was actually passed:
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:59:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1] #include <iostream>
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1] #include <string>
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1] int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1]     if (argc < 2) {
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1]         std::cerr << "Error: No argument provided.\n";
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1]         return 1;
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1]     }
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1]     std::string payload = argv[1];
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1]     payload += '\n';                    // Add newline at the end
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1]     // Example usage
2026-06-19 23:59:10 [grok-build-0.1]     std::cout << "Payload: " << payload;
