2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] - 1-bit vs 2-bit rendering paths (`monobit.` vs `pal576i.`)
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] - JSON-RPC control surface
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] - Host tooling (COM port enumeration, reset, echo, RPC client)
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] The resistor-DAC approach (using `pindirs` tricks to turn pins on/off into a crude DAC) is quite
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] aggressive.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] Notable observations
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Very sophisticated PIO chaining
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1]    The `pixelpump → onebit → threepin` pipeline is clever but fragile. Any change to line count,
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] pixel format, or timing constants can desynchronise the whole chain.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Duplication is getting painful
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1]    - `monobit.cpp` / `pal576i.cpp` have near-duplicate `plot`/`rect`/`text`/`drawC64Char` families.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1]    - `flipBits` vs `flipBits2` + `initFlipBits2`.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1]    - Two completely separate framebuffers and drawing paths (`evenFrame`/`oddFrame` vs
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] progressive).
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Timing constants are scattered
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1]    - `vidbit.pio` has `HSYNC_WAIT29`, `BACKPORCH_WAIT32`, etc.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1]    - `pal576i.cpp` has its own preamble counts, sync pulse values, and line counts.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1]    - Some of these are duplicated or slightly different between 288p and 576i.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Host tooling is actually useful
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1]    The `tools/` stuff (especially `enumports.cpp` + `resetcoms.cpp` / `echocoms.cpp`) is practical.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] Detecting VID `2E8A` and blasting `\nBOOT\n` over all matching COM ports is a nice dev workflow.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 5. Some things are still stubs
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1]    - `receiveBuffer()` in `pal576i.cpp` is empty.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1]    - Several `#ifdef` paths (WST, COLORTRAIN, ONEBIT, etc.) are only partially wired.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] Questions / areas worth discussing
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] - Are you trying to get full 2-bit colour working via the resistor DAC, or are you mostly
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] validating the 1-bit path first?
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] - The current `pixelpump + onebit + threepin` design is very specific to 1-bit pixels with two
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] precomputed wave trains. Do you want to keep that model, or move toward a more general "pump N bits
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] per pixel" design?
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] - How married are you to the current split between `monobit.` and `pal576i.`? Consolidating
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] the drawing primitives would make maintenance much easier.
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] - Clock is 166.5 MHz for exact PAL subcarrier math — are you planning to support other system
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] clocks later, or is this locked?
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] What would be most useful right now?
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] Pick one (or suggest something else):
2026-06-20 01:39:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Review the video pipeline (`pixelpump` → `onebit` → `threepin` + chroma) for timing
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] hazards or simplification opportunities.
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Help consolidate the two rendering paths (1-bit vs 2-bit) into a cleaner abstraction.
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Audit the PIO timing constants and see if we can derive more of them from a single
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] source of truth.
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Look at the host tools — improve VID/PID matching, add proper close handling, or make
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] the RPC side more robust.
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] 5. Something specific (e.g. "why is my chroma burst not locking", "how do I add proper
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] interlaced field handling", "teletext insertion plan", etc.).
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] Just tell me which direction you want to go. The project has grown into something quite
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] substantial.
2026-06-20 01:40:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:02 [nitro@ryzen5] in js how do a remove a key from an object?
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.9.0 ⛲  grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.298 819.6KB 7.50s
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] • In JavaScript, the most common ways to remove a key from an object are:*
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 1. `delete` (Mutates the original object)
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] const user = {
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1]   name: "Simon",
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1]   age: 35,
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1]   city: "London"
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] };
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] delete user.age;
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] console.log(user);
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] // { name: "Simon", city: "London" }
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] • Notes:*
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] - `delete` returns `true` if the deletion was successful.
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] - It mutates the object.
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] - It only works on own properties (not inherited ones).
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] - Avoid using `delete` on arrays — it creates holes.
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Destructuring + Rest (Recommended for immutability)
2026-06-20 01:44:10 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] If you don’t want to mutate the original object:
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] const user = {
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1]   name: "Simon",
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1]   age: 35,
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1]   city: "London"
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] };
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] const { age, ...newUser } = user;
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] console.log(newUser);
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] // { name: "Simon", city: "London" }
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] console.log(user); // original is unchanged
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] This is the modern, preferred approach in most cases (especially with React, Redux, etc.).
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Remove multiple keys
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] const user = { name: "Simon", age: 35, city: "London", country: "UK" };
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] const { age, country, ...newUser } = user;
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] console.log(newUser);
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] // { name: "Simon", city: "London" }
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Using a helper function (clean reusable way)
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] function removeKey(obj, key) {
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1]   const { [key]: _, ...rest } = obj;
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1]   return rest;
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] }
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] const user = { name: "Simon", age: 35, city: "London" };
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] const newUser = removeKey(user, "age");
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] console.log(newUser); // { name: "Simon", city: "London" }
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] Summary
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] ╭─────────────────────────────┬────────────────────┬────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────╮
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] │  Method                     │  Mutates Original  │  Modern / Recommended  │  Best For                      │
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] ├─────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] │  `delete obj.key`           │  Yes               │  No                    │  Simple mutation               │
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] │  `{ key, ...rest } = obj`   │  No                │  **Yes**               │  Most cases (immutable)        │
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] │  Helper function            │  No                │  **Yes**               │  Reusable code                 │
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] ╰─────────────────────────────┴────────────────────┴────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────╯
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] • Recommendation:* Use the destructuring method unless you specifically need to mutate the
2026-06-20 01:44:11 [grok-build-0.1] original object.
