2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] Let's talk architecture for running the three Discord bots cleanly.
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] Current situation (from what I can see)
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] You have:
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] - One central `relay.ts` process acting as a hub.
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] - Multiple separate `slopbot.ts` processes (foggy, sloppy, riff), each connecting to the hub over
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] TCP (port 8081).
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] - A broadcast mechanism that pushes messages out from the relay to connected bots.
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] - The bots appear to be reacting to the same events/triggers, resulting in unison behavior.
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] This is a classic hub + multiple clients pattern, but right now it sounds like the hub is
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] treating all connected bots as equivalent listeners rather than distinct agents.
2026-06-20 09:53:45 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] Key architectural questions we should resolve
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Routing vs Broadcasting
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - Currently it seems like most messages are being broadcast to all bots.
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - Do you want the relay to act as a smart router (deciding which bot(s) should receive a
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] message), or should it stay as a dumb message bus where the bots themselves decide whether to act?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Bot Identity & Addressing
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - How should users (or the system) target a specific bot?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - Discord @mentions?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - Prefixes in the message?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - Dedicated channels per bot?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - Bot-specific commands?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - Should the bots be able to address each other directly?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Decision Authority
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - Where should the logic live for "should this bot respond?"
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - In the central relay (centralized control)?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - In each bot process (decentralized, each bot filters for itself)?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - A mix (e.g. relay does coarse routing, bots do fine filtering)?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Conversation Context
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - Should each bot maintain its own independent history/context?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - Or should there be a shared session that all three bots can see?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - Or something in between (shared project state, but per-bot conversation state)?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 5. Message Types
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - Are there different categories of messages that need different handling?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - User messages directed at the system
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - System announcements
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - Tool results / internal events
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - Bot-to-bot communication
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]      - Commands
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 6. Connection Model
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - One relay process managing multiple bot connections (current direction).
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - Should each bot still be a completely separate process, or could some be collapsed later?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1]    - How do you want to handle bot startup/shutdown and reconnection?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] Likely cause of "react in unison"
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] From the shape of the current code, the most probable reasons are:
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Broadcasts are sent to every connected client without routing metadata.
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] - The bots don't have strong enough identity or filtering logic when they receive a message.
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] - The main relay doesn't tag messages with an intended recipient.
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] Before we go deeper, I’d like to understand your intent:
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] - What is the desired behavior when a user posts a message in the Discord channel(s)?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Should the three bots have distinct personalities/roles, or are they mostly interchangeable right
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] now?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Do you want users to be able to choose which bot they’re talking to, or should the system decide?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Is there any scenario where you want more than one bot to respond to the same message?
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] Reply with your thoughts on any of the above (or add your own constraints), and we can sketch out a
2026-06-20 09:53:46 [grok-build-0.1] cleaner architecture.
