2026-06-20 09:58:49 [nitro@ryzen5] ok history of discussion is longer lived than bot instances, does that help
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.113 349.2KB 9.27s
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] • Yes, that's a helpful and important constraint.* It changes the shape of the architecture
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] meaningfully.
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] What this tells us
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] - The relay is the long-lived owner of conversation history and project state.
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] - The bots are more transient — they can restart, reconnect, or be added/removed without
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] losing the discussion.
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] - Bots should be treated more like frontends / interfaces rather than stateful
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] participants.
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] This leans the design toward the relay being the source of truth, and the bots being
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] relatively lightweight connectors between Discord and the relay.
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] Implications for the current problem (unison responses)
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] This helps clarify a few things:
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 1. @mention filtering becomes cleaner
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]    Since history lives in the relay, the bots don't need to maintain their own view of the
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] conversation to decide whether to respond. They can make a simple local decision ("Was I
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] mentioned?") and only forward relevant messages.
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Bots can be simpler
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]    They don't need to carry much context themselves. Their main jobs are:
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]    - Listen for Discord events
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]    - Forward @mentions + commands to the relay
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]    - Take messages from the relay and post them to Discord
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]    - Identify themselves clearly when they connect
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Routing can live in the relay
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]    Because the relay has the full history and context, it becomes a natural place to make decisions
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] like:
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]    - Which bot should reply
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]    - Whether a message needs special handling
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]    - Maintaining per-bot personas or model assignments (if you go that way later)
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Reconnection is simpler
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1]    A bot can reconnect and immediately be useful without having to sync history. The relay doesn't
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] have to treat bot restarts as significant events for the conversation.
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:01 [grok-build-0.1] Questions this raises
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] Given that the relay owns the long-lived state, here are some useful things to decide:
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] - Bot identity on connect
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1]   Should a bot announce its name (e.g. "I am sloppy") when it connects to the relay? This would let
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] the relay know exactly which Discord bot is on which connection.
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] - Message tagging
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1]   When the relay sends something back to Discord via a bot, does it need to know which bot
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] to use, or is there one "main" bot for output?
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] - Commands vs chat
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1]   Since history lives in the relay, should commands (like `/reset`, `/model`, `/push`) also be
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] processed centrally? This would mean even command messages probably need to be forwarded when they
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] arrive via a bot.
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] - Visibility of non-@ messages
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1]   Even if bots only act on @mentions, should plain messages still be forwarded to the relay
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] so they can be recorded in history? Or should they be dropped at the bot level?
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] - Bot differentiation
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1]   Do you see the three bots as having their own persistent identities from the relay's perspective
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] (e.g. each bot might eventually be tied to a specific model or persona), or are they currently just
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] interchangeable ways to talk to the same underlying system?
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] This constraint (relay owns history, bots are shorter-lived) is a good foundation. It makes the
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] "@mention only" rule easier to implement cleanly.
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like to lock in a direction on any of the points above, or is there another aspect of the
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] longer-lived history that you think is important to consider?
2026-06-20 09:59:02 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:15 [nitro@ryzen5] it feels like obfiscation is happening, where is a bot reply being generated and emitted?
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.114 352.8KB 9.81s
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] • Clear answer:*
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] A bot reply is generated in one place and emitted in another.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] Generation (where the actual text is created)
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] The model reply is generated inside `relay.ts`.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] Specifically:
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] - In the `relay(depth, from)` function.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] - After the call to the model provider (`endpoint.chat.completions.create(...)` or the equivalent
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] for Anthropic/Gemini/etc.).
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] - The text comes from `choice.message.content` (or the `responses` endpoint equivalent).
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] - Once the content is received, it is:
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1]   - Added to `rohaHistory` as an assistant message.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1]   - Passed to `slopBroadcast(...)`.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] At this point, no Discord message has been sent yet. The relay only has the text.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] Emission to Discord (where it actually appears in the channel)
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] The text is sent to Discord inside `slopbot.ts`.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] The flow is:
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 1. `slopBroadcast()` in `relay.ts` writes the message over the TCP connection(s).
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 2. The receiving `slopbot.ts` gets it in its read loop.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 3. It calls `onFountain(message)`.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 4. `onFountain` calls `messageSloppy(message, userId)`.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 5. `messageSloppy` does the actual `channel.send(...)` to Discord.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] Why it currently feels obfuscated
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] This split is the main source of the confusion:
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] - Generation = relay process (central, long-lived, owns history)
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] - Emission = bot process (short-lived, one per Discord connection)
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] Because the relay currently does a broadcast to every connected bot, all three bots
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] (`sloppy`, `riff`, `foggy`) receive the same generated content and all call `messageSloppy`, which
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] is why they all reply.
2026-06-20 10:00:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] There is also no strong tagging on the broadcast that says “this message is intended for this
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] specific bot”.
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] Summary of the current path for a reply
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] User message in Discord
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] → `slopbot.ts` (messageCreate)
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] → TCP to relay
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] → `relay.ts` (added to history + `relay()` called)
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] → Model generates text
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] → `slopBroadcast()` (to all bots)
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] → `slopbot.ts` (onFountain → messageSloppy)
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] → Discord `channel.send()`
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] Does this match what you were seeing, or do you think there's another path the replies are taking
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] that I haven't surfaced yet?
2026-06-20 10:00:30 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:16 [nitro@ryzen5] i think we should add tagging from main relay
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.115 355.2KB 8.48s
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] • Good direction.*
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] Adding tagging from the main relay makes sense given the constraints we've established (relay owns
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] long-lived history, bots are shorter-lived interfaces, and bots should only act on @mentions).
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] What "tagging from the relay" would mean
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] Instead of the relay doing a pure broadcast to every connected bot, it would attach a recipient
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] tag (or set of tags) when it sends messages outward over TCP.
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] This shifts responsibility for routing decisions to the relay, where the context and history live.
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] Key things this enables
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] - The relay can decide which specific bot should handle a message, based on the incoming
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] @mention.
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] - Bots can become passive listeners: they only process and emit to Discord if the tag matches their
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] identity.
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] - It creates a cleaner separation:
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]   - Relay = decision maker + state owner
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]   - Bots = delivery mechanism + identity filter
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] Areas we should discuss before going further
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] Here are the main architectural questions this raises:
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 1. What form should the tag take?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - Simple bot name (`"sloppy"`, `"riff"`, `"foggy"`)?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - Something more structured (e.g. a target field, a list of targets, or a special "all" value)?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - Should system-generated messages (tool results, status, model replies) be tagged differently
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] from user-triggered replies?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 2. How does the relay know who to tag?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - Primarily driven by the @mention in the original Discord message (passed up from the bot)?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - Does the relay need to maintain a mapping of bot names to active connections?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - What happens if the mentioned bot is not currently connected?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Default / fallback behavior
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - If a message arrives with no @mention (even if we later decide to still forward some of them
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] for history), what tag does the relay use?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - Should there be a concept of a "default" or "primary" bot?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Commands
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - Should commands also carry a target tag, or are they treated as system-wide?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - If a command comes in via `@sloppy /reset`, should the tag be used to decide which bot
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] acknowledges the result?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 5. Messages originating in the relay
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - Things like model completions, tool call results, or internal announcements still need to
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] reach Discord.
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - Should these be tagged to a specific bot, or is there a different path/mechanism for
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] "relay-initiated" output?
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] 6. Multiple targets
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1]    - Do we need to support tagging a message to more than one bot at once (if someone @mentions two
2026-06-20 10:01:27 [grok-build-0.1] bots)?
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] This approach feels like it reduces the "obfuscation" you mentioned earlier, because the relay
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] becomes explicit about intent when it emits messages.
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] Quick questions to sharpen this
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] - Do you want the tagging to be strict (if the tag doesn't match the bot, it drops the
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] message completely)?
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] - Should the relay also tag incoming messages from bots (so it knows which bot originated a
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] user message or command)?
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] - Are you thinking of the tag as something that travels with every message the relay sends
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] outward, or only certain types?
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] Let me know how you want to narrow this down. We can keep refining the flow before any
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] implementation details.
2026-06-20 10:01:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:07 [nitro@ryzen5] mentions as a string array of names is simplest i think
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.116 358.6KB 10.04s
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] • Got it.*
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] Using a string array of names for mentions (`mentions: ["sloppy", "riff"]`) is a simple and
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] explicit approach. It keeps the tagging lightweight while still allowing the relay to express
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] intent clearly.
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] How this could work conceptually
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] - When the relay sends a message outward (via the current broadcast mechanism), it includes a
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] `mentions` array.
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Each bot receives the message and checks: "Is my name in the `mentions` array?"
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]   - If yes → process and emit to Discord.
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]   - If no → ignore.
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] - This gives the relay control over who should act, without the bots having to do complex
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] filtering.
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] This fits well with the earlier decisions:
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Relay owns the long-lived state and can build the `mentions` array with full context.
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Bots stay relatively dumb about routing.
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] Things this decision surfaces
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] Here are the main areas we should clarify:
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Source of the array
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - The array would primarily come from parsing the original Discord @mentions in the incoming
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] message.
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - Should the relay ever add names to the array on its own (for example, for system
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] messages or when it wants to force a specific bot to speak)?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Messages with no mentions
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - What should the array look like for messages that have no @mentions?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]      - Empty array `[]`?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]      - A special value like `["*"]` or `"all"`?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - Related: even if we drop non-mention user messages at the bot level, some internal messages
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] from the relay will still need to reach Discord.
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Relay-generated replies
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - When the relay produces a model completion (the actual reply text), what should go in the
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] `mentions` array?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]      - The bot(s) that were mentioned in the original user message?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]      - A single responsible bot?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]      - Something else?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Commands
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - If someone does `@sloppy /reset`, should the command message carry `mentions: ["sloppy"]`?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - Should command results (acknowledgments, errors, etc.) also be tagged back to the same bot?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 5. Multiple mentions
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - If a user @mentions two bots, the array would contain both names.
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - Are you comfortable with both bots potentially replying to the same message in that case?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 6. Bot identity
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - This approach assumes the bots have stable, known names (`"sloppy"`, `"riff"`, `"foggy"`).
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - Should the relay maintain a list of currently connected bots so it can validate or normalize
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] names?
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:18 [grok-build-0.1] 7. System / internal traffic
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1]    - How should we handle messages that the relay wants to send to Discord that aren't direct
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] replies to a user (tool results, status updates, announcements)?
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1]    - Should they use a different mechanism, or also go through the same `mentions` tagging?
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] Quick clarification questions
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] - Do you see the `mentions` array as something that is always present on messages going
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] from relay → bots, or only on certain types of messages?
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] - For a normal user @mention (e.g. `@sloppy explain this`), what would the ideal `mentions` array
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] look like from the relay’s perspective?
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] - What should happen with messages that originate inside the relay (not triggered by a Discord
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] user)? Should they carry an empty array, a special tag, or use a different path entirely?
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] Let me know which parts of this feel solid and which need more discussion.
2026-06-20 10:03:19 [grok-build-0.1] 
