2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.039 121.3KB 8.04s
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] The error is specifically about implicit int on the definition of `sysini()`.
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] Why this error appears
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] Modern compilers (C99 and later, especially with `-Werror` or strict warning settings) no longer
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] allow a function definition to start without an explicit return type. When the compiler sees:
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] sysini()
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] it assumes the return type is `int` by default. This is the old pre-ANSI "implicit int" rule that
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] was removed from the language.
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] Context in this file
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] - Earlier in the file there is already a forward declaration that correctly specifies `void
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] sysini();`
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] - The actual definition (around line 469) is written in pure K&R style without repeating the return
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] type.
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] - This creates a mismatch between the declaration and the definition that modern compilers now
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] reject.
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] Nature of the function
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] `sysini()` performs setup work (opening the game file, determining memory, setting up the save
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] filename) and does not return any value. The call site in `main()` also treats it as a void
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] function.
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] This is one of the first of many similar legacy patterns in this codebase, since it originates from
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] 1986-era C.
2026-06-20 12:27:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
