2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] • No, you cannot give `getvar()` a default argument.*
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] This is C11 (as set in your CMakeLists.txt). Default arguments are a C++-only feature and do not
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] exist in any version of C.
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] What is actually happening with getvar()
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] The current issue is almost certainly not a call site that is missing an argument. The real
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] problem is this line inside `nxtins()`:
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1] short argcount,
2026-06-20 12:39:04 [grok-build-0.1]       ts1, getvar();   /* temp for signed operations */
2026-06-20 12:39:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:05 [grok-build-0.1] In the original 1986 K&R style, this was a (very old) way of mixing a variable declaration with a
2026-06-20 12:39:05 [grok-build-0.1] forward function declaration in the same statement. Modern C (especially C11) no longer allows this
2026-06-20 12:39:05 [grok-build-0.1] syntax. The compiler now treats the `getvar();` part as invalid in a variable declaration context.
2026-06-20 12:39:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:05 [grok-build-0.1] This is why you're seeing an error attached to `getvar()` right now.
