2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.071 226.5KB 9.98s
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] • No default argument would make sense here.*
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] Why
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] `getvar()` is a function that must be told which variable to read. It takes a
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] `ZIPBYT` (the variable number):
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] - `0` = top of Z-stack
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] - `1–15` = local variables
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] - `16+` = globals
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] There is no logical "default" value that would be safe or meaningful. Every call site already
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] passes an explicit argument (e.g. `getvar(argblk[1])`).
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] What the old line was actually doing
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] This line:
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] short argcount,
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1]       ts1, getvar();   /* temp for signed operations */
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] was not trying to call `getvar` with a default. In 1986 K&R C it was a weird old way of
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] declaring two things at once:
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] - `ts1` as a `short` variable, and
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] - `getvar` as a function that returns `short`
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:55 [grok-build-0.1] Modern C (C11) rejects this syntax completely. It is not a missing-argument problem — it is an
2026-06-20 12:39:56 [grok-build-0.1] illegal declaration style.
2026-06-20 12:39:56 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:56 [grok-build-0.1] Summary
2026-06-20 12:39:56 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:56 [grok-build-0.1] - You cannot add a default argument (C does not support them).
2026-06-20 12:39:56 [grok-build-0.1] - Even if it did, `getvar()` should never have one, because the argument is required.
2026-06-20 12:39:56 [grok-build-0.1] - The fix is to separate the variable declaration from the function prototype.
2026-06-20 12:39:56 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:39:56 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like to talk about the cleanest way to restructure that particular declaration area, or
2026-06-20 12:39:56 [grok-build-0.1] shall we wait for the exact compiler error you're seeing now?
