2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.072 229.5KB 6.99s
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] • Yes, let's discuss the cleanest way to restructure that area.*
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] The core problem in that line
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] Inside `nxtins()`, this declaration:
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] short argcount,
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1]       ts1, getvar();   /* temp for signed operations */
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] is doing two completely different things in one statement:
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Declaring local variables (`argcount` and `ts1`).
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Trying to forward-declare the function `getvar()` in the ancient K&R style.
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] Modern C (C11) does not allow function declarations to be mixed into variable declarations like
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] this. That is why the compiler is currently complaining about `getvar()` at this location.
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] What should happen instead
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] Since you already added a proper prototype near the top of the file:
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] short getvar(ZIPBYT);
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] that prototype is now visible to the whole translation unit (including inside `nxtins()`). You no
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] longer need any kind of declaration for `getvar()` inside the function.
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] The cleanest approach is to completely separate the two concerns:
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] - Keep only the actual local variables as a normal declaration.
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] - Remove the old-style function declaration part entirely.
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] - Leave the prototype where it already is (at file scope), because that is the correct modern place
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] for it.
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] Additional context
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] This pattern (cramming function declarations into the middle of variable lists) appears in a few
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] other places in the original code. Fixing this one instance will almost certainly reveal the next
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] similar case once you recompile.
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] Also note that the actual definition of `getvar()` still uses the old K&R two-line style.
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] That is a separate (but related) issue that will likely need attention soon after the declaration
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] problem is resolved.
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:40:29 [grok-build-0.1] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
