2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.073 231.7KB 6.73s
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] • No, you should not use address-of function syntax (`&getvar`) inside `nxtins()`.*
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] Why that is not the right direction
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] The line that is currently causing trouble:
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] short argcount,
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1]       ts1, getvar();   /* temp for signed operations */
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] was never about taking the address of the function. In the old K&R style it was just a very sloppy
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] way of declaring two things in one statement:
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] - `ts1` as a local `short` variable (used to hold a temporary signed value for the `OPQDLE` and
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] `OPQIGR` cases)
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] - `getvar` as a function returning `short`
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] It was not creating a function pointer or taking the address of anything.
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] Current situation
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] Because you already added this at file scope:
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] short getvar(ZIPBYT);
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] the function is now properly visible. Inside `nxtins()` you can simply call it the normal way:
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] ts1 = getvar(argblk[1]);
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] There is no need (and no benefit) to write `&getvar`, `(*getvar)`, or anything similar here.
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] `getvar` is not being passed around as a function pointer in this code — it is just being called
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] directly.
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] What `ts1` actually needs
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] `ts1` is just a plain temporary local variable. It does not need to be connected to the function
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] declaration at all anymore.
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] Recommendation
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] The cleanest fix is to remove the old mixed declaration entirely and treat `ts1` as a normal local
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] variable. The function `getvar` should stay declared only via the prototype you already placed near
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] the top of the file.
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] Using address-of syntax would be a misunderstanding of what the original line was doing, and it
2026-06-20 12:41:28 [grok-build-0.1] would not solve the underlying problem (the illegal K&R mixed declaration syntax).
2026-06-20 12:41:29 [grok-build-0.1] 
