2026-06-20 12:45:18 [nitro@ryzen5] now help with return values in putvar, i dont understand
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.099 333.0KB 17.24s
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] • Yes, let's talk about `putvar` and return values.*
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] `putvar` is a setter — its only job is to store a value somewhere (global, local, or top of
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] stack). It is not supposed to return anything to the caller. That is why you see bare
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] `return;` statements inside it: they just mean “exit the function now, I have finished the set
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] operation”.
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] The current confusion
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] Because the function is still written in the old style (`putvar(var, value)` with no return type in
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] front), the compiler treats it as if it returns an `int`. This creates a mismatch in the compiler’s
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] mind between “this function has no return value” (the `return;` lines) and “this function is
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] supposed to return an int”.
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] That is almost certainly what is triggering the warning or error you are seeing about return
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] values.
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] Additional problem in the stack case
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] There is also a clear logic bug in the branch that handles the stack (when `var` is 0). In that
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] branch the code is using the variable number instead of the actual value you want to store. This
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] means the value parameter is effectively being ignored for top-of-stack stores. That is likely
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] adding to your confusion about “return values”.
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] What needs to happen conceptually
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 1. The function should be declared as returning nothing.
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 2. The prototype (if you add one) and the definition must agree on that.
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 3. The stack-top assignment needs to actually use the `value` that was passed in.
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like to discuss the cleanest way to mark the function as returning nothing (both at the
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] prototype level and at the definition level), or shall we focus first on why the stack case is
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] mishandling the value that is being “put”?
2026-06-20 12:45:38 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:37:03 [nitro@ryzen5] how to pass arguments in deno.json
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣 0.7° $0.001 490B 12.15s
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] To pass arguments to a task defined in your deno.json file, invoke the task using the `deno task`
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] command followed by a double dash (`--`) separator. Anything after the `--` gets forwarded to the
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] underlying command or script.
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] For example:
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] `deno task yourTaskName -- arg1 arg2 value=123`
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] Those arguments will be available inside your script (typically via `Deno.args`).
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] If you need fixed/default arguments instead of dynamic ones, include them directly inside the task
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] definition string in the deno.json file.
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] If this doesn't match what you meant by "pass arguments" (e.g. something else like compiler flags
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] or environment variables), provide more details.
2026-06-21 07:37:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:39:09 [nitro@ryzen5] how to read in deno app ?
2026-06-21 07:39:15 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣 0.7° $0.001 1.387KB 5.35s
2026-06-21 07:39:15 [grok-build-0.1] • To read arguments in a Deno app*, use `Deno.args`.
2026-06-21 07:39:15 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:39:15 [grok-build-0.1] Basic Example
2026-06-21 07:39:15 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:39:15 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:39:15 [grok-build-0.1] // main.ts
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] console.log("All arguments:", Deno.args);
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] console.log("First argument:", Deno.args[0]);
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] console.log("Second argument:", Deno.args[1]);
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] How to run it
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] If you're using `deno task` (from your previous question):
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:39:16 [grok-build-0.1] // deno.json
