2026-06-21 07:49:28 [nitro@ryzen5] use args in bat file
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣 0.7° $0.001 476B 11.71s
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] To use command-line arguments in a Windows batch file (.bat), reference them with `%1` for the
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] first argument, `%2` for the second, and so on (up to `%9`). Use `shift` if you need more than 9.
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] Always validate inputs to avoid errors.
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] Here's a basic example:
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] @echo off
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] if "%1"=="" (
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] 	echo Usage: simonsta.bat [name] [age]
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] 	exit /b 1
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] )
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] echo Name: %1
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] echo Age: %2
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] rem Example: Use a named variable for reuse
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] set name=%1
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] echo Hello, %name%!
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] Save as `simonsta.bat` and run it like this:
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] `simonsta.bat John 25`
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] This will output:
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] Name: John
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] Age: 25
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] Hello, John!
2026-06-21 07:49:41 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:01 [nitro@ryzen5] is ther a 5 for all of them together
2026-06-21 07:50:33 [nitro@ryzen5] a way to echo all args
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣 0.7° $0.001 1.332KB 10.30s
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] Yes, use `%*` to refer to all arguments together.
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] Here's the simple way to echo them all at once:
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] @echo off
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] echo All arguments: %*
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] Example usage:
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] Run this:
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] simonsta.bat hello world 42 test
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] It will output:
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] All arguments: hello world 42 test
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] Other common uses of `%*`:
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Pass all arguments to another program:
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1]   another.bat %*
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Pass all arguments plus the script name:
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1]   echo %0 %*
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] - Use it inside a command:
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1]   echo First arg is %1 and the rest are: %*
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like a version that loops through each argument individually instead?
2026-06-21 07:50:46 [grok-build-0.1] 
