2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] rem Run your command using the saved path as an argument
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] mycommand.exe "%current_dir%"
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] rem Return to the original directory
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] popd
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] endlocal
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] Why the change?
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] - `setlocal` without `enabledelayedexpansion` is enough here.
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] - Using `%current_dir%` is the normal and safer way when you don't need delayed expansion.
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] - `!current_dir!` only works if you enable delayed expansion, which adds complexity and can behave
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] unexpectedly with certain characters in paths.
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] When you would use `!` instead:
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] You only need delayed expansion if the variable is being read inside a `for` loop or a
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] parenthesized block. For this simple pattern (save dir → pushd → run command), `%var%` is cleaner.
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 08:40:05 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like a version that also handles paths with spaces or special characters more robustly?
