2026-06-21 09:04:23 [nitro@ryzen5] in bat what was set to evaluate in order
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.001 496B 12.87s
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] • In .bat files (Windows batch scripting), this is handled with `SETLOCAL
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] ENABLEDELAYEDEXPANSION`.*
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] Why it exists
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] By default, batch files expand variables (`%var%`) at parse time (when the line is read),
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] not when the line actually runs. This means inside loops, `if` blocks, or after a variable is
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] changed, the value stays frozen at the original parse-time value.
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] To make variables evaluate in execution order (i.e. when the line is actually reached), you
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] turn on delayed expansion:
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] SETLOCAL ENABLEDELAYEDEXPANSION
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] SET count=0
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] FOR %%A IN (1 2 3) DO (
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1]     SET /A count+=1
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1]     ECHO !count!          REM <- note the ! ! instead of % %
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] )
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] ENDLOCAL
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] Key points
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] - Use `!variable!` (exclamation marks) for delayed variables.
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] - Use `%variable%` for normal (parse-time) expansion.
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] - `SETLOCAL ENABLEDELAYEDEXPANSION` must be inside the script (or called from a parent).
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] - Always pair it with `ENDLOCAL` when you want to restore the previous state.
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] If this isn't what you meant by "set to evaluate in order", give me a bit more context (e.g. a code
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] snippet or the exact behaviour you're seeing) and I'll refine it.
2026-06-21 09:04:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
