2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.3 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.034 106.3KB 5.96s
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] • No, Windows does not* have `SIGQUIT`.
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] Details:
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] - `SIGQUIT` is a POSIX/Unix signal (usually signal 3).
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] - On Unix it is typically triggered by `Ctrl+\`.
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] - Windows has very limited signal support. The standard `signal()` in `<signal.h>` only properly
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] supports these:
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] ╭──────────────┬────────────────────┬───────────────╮
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] │  Signal      │  Windows support   │  Notes        │
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] ├──────────────┼────────────────────┼───────────────┤
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] │  `SIGINT`    │  Yes               │  Ctrl+C       │
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] │  `SIGTERM`   │  Limited           │  -            │
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] │  `SIGABRT`   │  Yes               │  -            │
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] │  `SIGFPE`    │  Yes               │  -            │
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] │  `SIGILL`    │  Yes               │  -            │
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] │  `SIGSEGV`   │  Yes               │  -            │
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] │  `SIGQUIT`   │  **No**            │  Not defined  │
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] ╰──────────────┴────────────────────┴───────────────╯
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:18:59 [grok-build-0.1] If you compile the original line:
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] signal(SIGQUIT, z_exit);
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] on Windows (even with MinGW or MSVC), you will get an error because `SIGQUIT` is not defined.
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] Recommended approach for your modernization
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] Since you're aiming for platform-agnostic modern C, here’s the cleanest way to handle it:
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] #ifdef _WIN32
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1]     signal(SIGINT, z_exit);
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1]     /* SIGQUIT does not exist on Windows */
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] #else
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1]     signal(SIGINT, z_exit);
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1]     signal(SIGQUIT, z_exit);
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] #endif
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] Or, if you want it a bit more structured, you can wrap it in a helper:
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] static void setup_signals(void)
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] {
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1]     signal(SIGINT, z_exit);
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] #ifndef _WIN32
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1]     signal(SIGQUIT, z_exit);
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] #endif
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] }
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] Bonus (better long-term option)
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] For Windows console apps, many people prefer `SetConsoleCtrlHandler` over `signal()` because it
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] gives more control. If you want to go that route later, I can show you a portable abstraction.
2026-06-21 10:19:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
