2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] - This is a 1986-era C implementation (adapted from Unix ZIP by Duncan Blanchard), targeting early
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] UNIX systems with AT&T 7300/PC-specific extensions (`tam.h`, windowing, alternate screen buffer for
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] block/icon graphics).
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] - It implements a full Z-machine (ZIP) with custom DIP opcodes for icons, blocksets, iteration
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] tables, clipping, input polling, and masked drawing.
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] - Structure is classic: memory paging/LRU, Z-stack, opcode dispatch in `nxtins()`, virtual memory
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] helpers (`getpag`, `getbyt`, etc.), and machine-dependent graphics layer.
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] - Preload strategy, save/restore, verify checksum, and restart handling are all present.
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] - Heavy use of macros for virtual memory access, predicates, and stack ops.
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] - Debug support is conditionally compiled (`_DEBUG`).
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] • Notable aspects (no major surprises vs. classic ZIP sources):*
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] - Graphics path uses an alternate screen bitmap (`altscreen`) + `md_drawblock` + `md_screen_update`
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] for speed.
2026-06-21 10:37:37 [grok-build-0.1] - Special `OPCALL` handling for up to 16 args (unusual but documented).
