2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] • Diagnosis of the error*
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] The crash comes from this line in `dip.c`:
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] lseek(gamechn, offset, 0);
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] if (read(gamechn, loc, BLKSIZ) == 0) THEN
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1]     fatal("Get block failed");
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] (See: `getblk()` called from `memini()` for block 0, and again for the picture block.)
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] The `open(gamfile, RDONLY)` succeeded (otherwise you would have seen "Failed to open game file"),
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] but the first 512-byte read returned 0 bytes.
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] Most likely causes (in order)
2026-06-21 10:56:08 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] 1. `..\foo.dat` is empty or too small
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1]    A valid Z-machine story file (even a tiny test) must be at least 512 bytes for the first block.
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1]    Run from the `bin` directory:
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1]    dir ..\foo.dat
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1]    If it shows 0 bytes (or doesn't exist), that's the problem.
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Windows binary-mode problem (very common with this old code)
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1]    The code does:
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1]    gamechn = open(gamfile, RDONLY);   // RDONLY == 0
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1]    On Windows (especially with MinGW/MSVC), `open()` defaults to text mode. This can cause:
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1]    - CR/LF translation
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1]    - Early EOF on binary data
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1]    - Short or zero-byte reads for the first block
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Missing error checking on `lseek`
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1]    `lseek` can fail silently here; the code never checks its return value.
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Less likely: the file is a valid story file but the image file header logic (`ibase =
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] GTVWRD(PLENTH) + IFUDGE`) is pointing at garbage because the first block read was already
2026-06-21 10:56:09 [grok-build-0.1] corrupted.
