2026-06-21 11:06:40 [nitro@ryzen5] should i map open to fopen?
2026-06-21 11:06:59 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.3 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.116 406.8KB 5.51s
2026-06-21 11:06:59 [grok-build-0.1] • No, you should not map `open` to `fopen`.*
2026-06-21 11:06:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:06:59 [grok-build-0.1] That is a common temptation, but it would be a bad idea in this codebase.
2026-06-21 11:06:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:06:59 [grok-build-0.1] Why it's a bad idea
2026-06-21 11:06:59 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:06:59 [grok-build-0.1] ╭───────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────╮
2026-06-21 11:06:59 [grok-build-0.1] │  `open()` (current code)          │  `fopen()`                      │  Problem                   │
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] ├───────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] │  Returns `int` (file descriptor)  │  Returns `FILE*`                │  Type mismatch everywhere  │
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] │  `read(fd, buf, n)`               │  `fread(buf, 1, n, fp)`         │  Different API             │
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] │  `write(fd, buf, n)`              │  `fwrite(...)`                  │  Different API             │
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] │  `lseek(fd, off, whence)`         │  `fseek(fp, off, whence)`       │  Different API             │
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] │  `creat()`                        │  `fopen(..., "wb")`             │  Different                 │
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] │  `close(fd)`                      │  `fclose(fp)`                   │  Different                 │
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] ╰───────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────╯
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] There are many places in `dip.c` that use the low-level calls (`getblk`, `wrtbyts`,
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] `rdbyts`, `sav_res`, etc.). A simple `#define open fopen` will explode with compile or runtime
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] errors.
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] Recommended approach
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] Keep using the low-level `open()` / `read()` style, but force binary mode on Windows.
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] Best minimal fix
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] In `dip.c`, change the `open()` call in `sysini()` (around line ~300):
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #ifdef _WIN32
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1]     #include <fcntl.h>   // for O_BINARY
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1]     gamechn = open(gamfile, O_RDONLY | O_BINARY);
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #else
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1]     gamechn = open(gamfile, RDONLY);
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #endif
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] You should also do it for the save file in `sav_res()`:
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] if (fcn == OPSAVE) THEN
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #ifdef _WIN32
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1]     savechn = open(fptr, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_BINARY, FMODE);
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #else
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1]     savechn = creat(fptr, FMODE);
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #endif
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] else
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #ifdef _WIN32
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1]     savechn = open(fptr, O_RDONLY | O_BINARY);
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #else
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1]     savechn = open(fptr, RDONLY);
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #endif
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] Even cleaner option (optional)
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] Add this near the top of the file (after the includes):
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #ifdef _WIN32
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #include <fcntl.h>
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #define OPEN_RDONLY   (O_RDONLY | O_BINARY)
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #define OPEN_WRONLY   (O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_BINARY)
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #else
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #define OPEN_RDONLY   RDONLY
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #define OPEN_WRONLY   0   /* not used directly */
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] #endif
2026-06-21 11:07:00 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] Then use:
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] gamechn = open(gamfile, OPEN_RDONLY);
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like me to give you the exact minimal diff for both the game file and the save file?
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] Or would you prefer a slightly more structured approach (a couple of helper functions like
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] `open_game_file()` and `open_save_file()`)?
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:07:01 [grok-build-0.1] Also: make sure `..\foo.dat` is actually a real (non-empty) story file before testing.
