2026-06-21 11:16:38 [nitro@ryzen5] in windows what open arguement for binary read only
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.3 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.073 237.7KB 6.15s
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] • Answer:*
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] On Windows, when using the low-level `open()` / `read()` functions (not `fopen`), you need:
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] O_RDONLY | O_BINARY
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] Recommended change
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] In your current code, `RDONLY` is defined as `0` in `dipdefs.h`. That works on Unix but is wrong
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] for Windows.
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] Replace this definition:
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] #define RDONLY 0
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] With this (in the Windows path):
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] #define RDONLY (O_RDONLY | O_BINARY)
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] Full context / where to put it
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] In `dipdefs.h`, you can do it like this:
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] #ifdef UNIX
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] #define RDONLY 0
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] #else
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] #include <fcntl.h>
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] #include <io.h>
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] #define RDONLY (O_RDONLY | O_BINARY)
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] #endif
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] Then the existing call should work:
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] gamechn = open(gamfile, RDONLY);
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] Important notes
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] - `O_BINARY` is required. Without it, Windows does newline translation (`\r\n` ↔ `\n`), which will
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] corrupt the game file (especially the image data section).
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] - Make sure you include `<fcntl.h>` (and `<io.h>` for MSVC/MinGW) before using these flags.
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] - The same issue can affect `savechn` if you ever create save files (you may want `O_WRONLY |
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] O_CREAT | O_BINARY` for saves).
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like me to show the minimal patch for both `RDONLY` and the save file open as well?
2026-06-21 11:16:53 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:30 [nitro@ryzen5] where do escpae screen things happen
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.3 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.060 227.6KB 5.73s
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] • Escape sequences for screen control* (ANSI/VT100-style cursor, clear, scroll, and
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] attribute handling) are emitted in a handful of places in `dip.c`.
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] Main locations (all in the machine-dependent section near the end)
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 1. `cls()`
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]    printf("\033[2J\033[H");   /* clear + home */
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 2. `locate(row, col)`
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]    sprintf(control, "\033[%1d;%1dH", row, col);
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]    printf("%s", control);
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 3. `hilite(attrib)`
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]    printf("\033[%1dm", num);
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 4. `mcrlf()` (windowed scroll + [MORE] handling)
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]    printf("\033[M");           /* delete line (scroll) */
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]    ...
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]    printf("\033[6D\033[K");    /* backspace + clear after [MORE] */
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 5. `md_getl()` (a few control sequences + FEEP)
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]    - `FEEP` is defined as `"\007"` (BEL).
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]    - Escape-key sequences from the keyboard are discarded here:
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]      case ESC: {
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]          while (getchar() != -1);   /* throw away escape sequences */
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]          printf(FEEP);
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]          break;
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1]      }
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] Context notes (from the shared files)
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] - These are used for the text/status line side of DIP, not the graphics side.
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] - The graphics path (`op_showicon`, `md_drawblock`, `md_screen_update`, `altscreen` buffer,
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] `rev_table`, etc.) bypasses the text escapes entirely and writes directly to the alternate screen
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] bitmap.
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] - The code still emits the `\033` sequences even in the non-UNIX (`#ifndef HAS_TERMIO`) build that
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] uses `GetStdHandle(STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE)`. It assumes the console supports ANSI escapes (common on
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] modern Windows, AT&T 7300 via TAM, etc.).
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] - No higher-level abstraction (e.g. a `put_control` or `term_move` wrapper) exists; the sequences
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] are inlined in the four functions above.
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:50 [grok-build-0.1] If you want a clean port or to factor these out, the four functions (`cls`, `locate`, `hilite`,
2026-06-21 11:42:51 [grok-build-0.1] `mcrlf`) are the only places that need to change. Let me know if you'd like me to extract them into
2026-06-21 11:42:51 [grok-build-0.1] named reusable routines or show the exact lines from the shared `dip.c`.
2026-06-21 11:42:51 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:42:51 [grok-build-0.1] Anything new or different in the other shared files (headers, build, etc.) that you'd like comments
2026-06-21 11:42:51 [grok-build-0.1] on?
2026-06-21 11:42:51 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:49:00 [nitro@ryzen5] i need a simple md_screen_update function
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.3 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.073 230.8KB 21.65s
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] Here's a simple, portable version of `md_screen_update` you can drop in:
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] /* Simple text-based md_screen_update */
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] void md_screen_update(unsigned short locx, unsigned short locy,
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1]                       unsigned short width, unsigned short height)
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] {
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 	short y, x, row;
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 	unsigned short *ptr;
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 	unsigned short bits;
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 	for (y = 0; y < height; y++) {
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 		locate(locy + y + 1, locx + 1);   /* 1-based cursor */
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 		for (x = 0; x < width; x++) {
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 			/* Start of this block's 8 rows in the altscreen buffer */
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 			ptr = altscreen + ((locy + y) * SCRNX2 * GBLEN) + (locx + x);
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 			bits = 0;
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 			for (row = 0; row < GBLEN; row++) {
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 				bits |= ptr[row * SCRNX2];
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 			}
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 			/* Simple on/off representation */
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 			if (bits != 0)
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 				putchar('#');
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 			else
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 				putchar(' ');
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 		}
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 	}
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] }
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] Notes:
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] - Uses the existing `locate()` and `altscreen` buffer.
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] - Each graphics block (8 bytes) is shown as a single character.
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] - `'#'` = block has pixels, `' '` = empty.
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] - This is deliberately minimal and will work in any terminal.
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like an even simpler stub version, or one that tries to show a bit more detail (e.g.
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] using different characters for density)?
2026-06-21 11:49:33 [grok-build-0.1] 
