2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] Here's what this version does differently:
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] How it works
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] Each 8×8 pixel block is split into a 2×2 grid of 4×4 sub-regions:
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] ┌──┬──┐
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] │..│::│   Each sub-region counts how many of its 16 pixels
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] ├──┼──┤   are set, then picks a character by density:
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] │++│oo│
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] └──┴──┘   ' ' = 0 pixels    '+' = 6-9
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2]           '.' = 1-2 pixels   'o' = 10-13
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2]           ':' = 3-5 pixels   '#' = 14-16
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] Key points
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] - 80 columns × 48 lines total output (fits standard terminal)
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] - Line buffer — each text line is built in `linebuf[]` before output, so it's one `fputs`
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] per line instead of per-character `putchar`
2026-06-21 11:56:09 [glm-5.2] - Partial updates — `locate()` positions at `locx * 2 + 1` so icon-only redraws land in the
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] right spot
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] - `density_to_char()` is a named reusable function (not inlined)
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] - Reads pixel bits from `altscreen` using the `rev_byte` bit layout: pixel `i` lives at bit `i*2`
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] in each `unsigned short`
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] Pixel extraction
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] The `rev_byte()` function doubles each bit, so to read pixel `i` (0=leftmost) from a short:
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] (sv >> (i * 2)) & 1
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] The sub-region indexing `px  2 + subx  4` covers:
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] - `subx=0`: pixels 0–3 (left half)
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] - `subx=1`: pixels 4–7 (right half)
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] Drop it in replacing your current `#ifndef HAS_URDATA` version and you should see shapes instead of
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] solid walls. Let me know how it looks.
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] Here's a version that renders each 8×8 block as 2 chars wide × 2 lines tall (80×48
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] display), using density characters built up in a line buffer:
2026-06-21 11:56:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:20 [nitro@ryzen5] win32 sleep for millis command
2026-06-21 12:02:35 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.3 glm-5.2 Z 🪣  0.7° $0.193 466.8KB 14.62s
2026-06-21 12:02:35 [glm-5.2] For the `md_delay()` function, you can use the Win32 `Sleep()` API. You'll need to include
2026-06-21 12:02:35 [glm-5.2] `<windows.h>` and replace the busy-wait loop.
2026-06-21 12:02:35 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:35 [glm-5.2] Minimal change to `md_delay()`
2026-06-21 12:02:35 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] #ifdef _WIN32
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] #include <windows.h>
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] #endif
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] void md_delay(int ticks)
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] {
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 	/* one tick = 1/60 second ≈ 16.67 ms */
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 	DWORD millis = (DWORD)ticks * 1000 / 60;
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 	Sleep(millis);
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] }
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] If you also want a generic millisecond sleep elsewhere
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] static void md_sleep_ms(DWORD millis)
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] {
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 	Sleep(millis);
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] }
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] Notes
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] - `Sleep(0)` yields the timeslice but returns immediately — fine for a zero-tick delay.
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] - `Sleep()` is not precise to the millisecond on Windows (typical granularity is ~15ms), but it's
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] far better than the current `while (j--)` spin loop.
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] - If you need sub-15ms precision you'd need `timeBeginPeriod(1)` from `winmm.lib`, but for DIP game
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] timing this is unnecessary.
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] Want me to fold this into a full updated `md_delay` / `md_user_delay` section for `dip.c`?
2026-06-21 12:02:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:02:59 [nitro@ryzen5] a leand and mean flag?
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.3 glm-5.2 Z 🪣  0.7° $0.195 468.0KB 13.75s
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] A `LEAN_AND_MEAN` flag is a clean way to strip out debugging, verbose output, and non-essential
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] features at compile time. Here's how to wire it into the project:
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] At the top of `dip.c`
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] #ifdef LEAN_AND_MEAN
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 	#undef _DEBUG
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 	#define _DEBUG 0
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 	#define ETRAP 0
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] #endif
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] In `dipdefs.h` (or at top of `dip.c`)
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] #ifndef LEAN_AND_MEAN
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 	#define ETRAP 1
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] #else
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 	#ifndef ETRAP
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 		#define ETRAP 0
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 	#endif
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] #endif
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] In `CMakeLists.txt`
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] option(LEAN_AND_MEAN "Strip debug and error trapping for a minimal build" OFF)
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] if(LEAN_AND_MEAN)
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 	add_compile_definitions(LEAN_AND_MEAN)
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] endif()
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] What it disables
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] ╭──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] │  Feature                                             │  Controlled by  │  Effect                                            │
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] │  Debugger (`debugger()`, `dinfo()`, `dump()`, etc.)  │  `_DEBUG`       │  Removes ~400 lines of debugger code               │
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] │  Error trapping checks                               │  `ETRAP`        │  Skips bounds checks in `showrow`, `md_drawblock`  │
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] │  Verbose printf in `verify()`, `fatal()`             │  `_DEBUG`       │  Quieter fatal errors                              │
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] Build usage
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] cmake -G "Ninja" .. -DLEAN_AND_MEAN=ON
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] Or just add it to your compile flags:
2026-06-21 12:03:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] cmake --build . -- -j 1
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] with `add_compile_options(-DLEAN_AND_MEAN)` in CMakeLists.txt.
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] What stays
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] - Full opcode dispatch (`nxtins`)
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] - Paging (`getpag`, `getblk`, LRU chain)
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] - Graphics (`op_showicon`, `md_drawblock`, `md_screen_update`)
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] - Save/restore (`sav_res`)
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] - Input (`md_input`, `md_getl`)
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] Want me to fold this into a full updated `CMakeLists.txt`?
2026-06-21 12:03:15 [glm-5.2] 
