2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] Good distinction. Let me walk through how bot replies actually flow and where the persona gets
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] lost.
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] • Bot message path:*
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 1. A Discord bot (e.g. sloppy) sends a message to relay via TCP on port 8081
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 2. `slopPrompt` receives it as `{messages:[{message:{content}, from:"sloppy"}]}`
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 3. In the `chat()` loop, `m.from` is captured and logged for the incoming message via
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] `logForge(content, m.from)` — so the bot's name does appear in the log for input
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 4. The content is pushed to `lines` with `["sloppy"] ` prefixed
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 5. But then `relay(0, from)` is called with `from=rohaUser` — the bot's identity is dropped
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] here
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 6. The model generates a reply, `print()` puts it in `printBuffer` tagged with `model:rohaModel`
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 7. `flush()` logs the reply as `[mut_name] #reply text` — no bot persona anywhere
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] • The gap:* Between step 3 and step 5, the bot's identity (`m.from`) is embedded in the
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] message text but not passed forward as a parameter. So when the reply is generated and logged,
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] there's no way to know which bot triggered it.
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] • What's needed:*
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] - When `response.messages` arrives, capture `m.from` (e.g. "sloppy") and carry it through as the
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] `from` argument to `relay()` instead of always using `rohaUser`
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] - Inside `relay()`, the `from` parameter is already available — it just needs to flow into the log.
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] A global like `relayPersonaTag` set at the top of `relay()` from `from` would let `flush()` pick it
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] up when logging `printBuffer` entries
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] - For console relay (no bot involved), `from` stays as `rohaUser` and the `/persona` setting fills
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] the same role
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] • One wrinkle:* `flush()` is called at multiple points in the cycle, not just after
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] `relay()` returns. The global would need to be set before `relay()` runs and cleared or reset after
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] `flush()` completes, so stale persona tags don't leak into unrelated log lines.
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] • Another consideration:* The `slopBroadcast` for assistant replies already uses `from` —
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] `"<4assistant>["+from+"]"+content`. If `from` carried the bot name, the broadcast back to the bot
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] would also be tagged correctly, which may help the bot route its response to the right Discord
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] channel.
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] Does this match your thinking? The core change is threading `m.from` through to `relay()` and then
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] into the log via a global that `flush()` reads.
2026-06-22 12:24:10 [glm-5.2] 
