2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.3 glm-5.2 Z 🪣  0.7° $0.214 505.6KB 60.17s
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] Now I can see the full picture. Let me trace the bot flow end to end.
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] • Incoming (Discord → Relay):*
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] In `relaybot.ts`, when a Discord user messages, the bot sends:
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] {"messages":[{"message":"hello","from":"skudmarks@discord"}]}
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] The bot's own name (`sloppy`) is not in the payload. It's only known to the bot process
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] itself via `botName=Deno.args[1]`.
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] In `relay.ts` `chat()`, `m.from` is `"skudmarks@discord"` — that gets logged for the input line via
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] `logForge(content, m.from)`. Good. But then `relay(0, from)` is called with `from=rohaUser` —
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] hardcoded, not `m.from`, and definitely not the bot name.
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] • Outgoing (Relay → Discord):*
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] In `relay()`, the reply is broadcast via `slopBroadcast("<4assistant>["+from+"]"+content, from)`.
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] Since `from` is `rohaUser`, the bot receives `from=rohaUser` and has no idea which bot should
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] respond.
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] In `relaybot.ts` `onFountain()`, the filter is:
2026-06-22 12:49:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] if(from=="slop" && botName!="foggy") continue;
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] So all bots receive all broadcasts, and only `from=="slop"` is filtered. Every bot posts every
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] reply.
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] • The core problem:*
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] The bot's persona name (`sloppy`, `riff`, `foggy`) never reaches relay. It exists only in
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] `relaybot.ts` as `botName` and `sloppyInfo[botName]`. The TCP payload carries the Discord user's
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] identity but not the bot's.
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] • Options to fix:*
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 1. Bot includes its name in the payload — e.g.
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] `{"messages":[{"message":"hello","from":"skudmarks@discord","bot":"sloppy"}]}`. Relay then threads
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] `bot` through to `relay()` as the persona tag for logging and broadcast routing.
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 2. Relay tracks connections — map each TCP connection to a bot name. The bot could announce
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] itself on connect (it already sends `{"action":"connect"}` — could become
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] `{"action":"connect","bot":"sloppy"}`).
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 3. Use `from` for the bot name — set `from` to `sloppy` in the payload instead of the
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] Discord user. But then you lose the Discord user's identity in the log, which seems worse.
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] Option 1 seems cleanest — minimal change to the payload, the bot name flows naturally through the
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] existing `messages` array, and relay can use it for both logging and broadcast routing. The Discord
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] user's identity is still preserved in `from`.
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] Which direction are you leaning?
2026-06-22 12:49:30 [glm-5.2] 
