2026-06-23 09:41:19 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪣  0.7° $0.160 360.7KB 10.96s
2026-06-23 09:41:19 [glm-5.2] You're welcome! The implementation looks good. One small note: `GetAsyncKeyState` returns a `SHORT`
2026-06-23 09:41:19 [glm-5.2] where the high bit (`0x8000`) means "currently pressed" and the low bit means "pressed since last
2026-06-23 09:41:19 [glm-5.2] call". Checking `!= 0` works fine for a quit condition since either way the user has hit escape. If
2026-06-23 09:41:19 [glm-5.2] you ever need to distinguish "held down" from "pressed once", masking with `& 0x8000` would be the
2026-06-23 09:41:19 [glm-5.2] way to go.
2026-06-23 09:41:19 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:42:02 [nitro@ryzen5] is there command to read all keys in single call
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪣  0.7° $0.162 361.3KB 12.13s
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] Yes — `GetKeyboardState()` fills a 256-byte array with the state of every virtual key in one call.
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] Each byte corresponds to a key code (0–255), and the high bit (`0x80`) means that key is currently
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] down.
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] bool isAnyKeyDown(){
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 	BYTE keys[256];
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 	if(!GetKeyboardState(keys)) return false;
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 	for(int i = 0; i < 256; i++){
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 		if(keys[i] & 0x80) return true;
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 	}
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 	return false;
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] }
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] To check a specific key from the array:
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] bool isEscapeDown(){
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 	BYTE keys[256];
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 	if(!GetKeyboardState(keys)) return false;
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 	return (keys[VK_ESCAPE] & 0x80) != 0;
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] }
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] One caveat: `GetKeyboardState` reflects the keyboard state as seen by the calling thread's message
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] queue, so it works best when your process has a console window with focus. `GetAsyncKeyState` polls
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] the hardware directly and works regardless of focus, so for a simple "press escape to quit" in a
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] console app, your current `GetAsyncKeyState` approach is actually the more reliable choice.
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] `GetKeyboardState` is the better pick when you want to read multiple keys simultaneously (e.g.
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] checking for key combinations or reading a full chord).
2026-06-23 09:42:15 [glm-5.2] 
