2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪣  0.7° $0.171 378.7KB 26.91s
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] The `MsgWaitForMultipleObjects` usage with 10ms is correct, but there are two things to be aware of
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] in a console app:
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] • 1. The `[MSG]` line will likely never print.* Console applications don't receive Win32
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] keyboard messages through the message queue. Console input goes through the console input buffer
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] (`ReadConsoleInput` / `stdin`), which is a completely separate subsystem. `PeekMessage` will find
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] nothing to pump.
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] • 2. `GetKeyboardState` will return stale or empty data* for the same reason — it reads
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] from the thread's message queue keyboard state, which never gets updated in a console app.
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] The reliable path for a console application is `GetAsyncKeyState` which polls the hardware
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] directly:
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] bool anyKeyDown(){
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 	for(int i = 0; i < 256; i++){
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 		if(GetAsyncKeyState(i) & 0x8000) return true;
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 	}
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 	return false;
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] }
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] With that you can drop `pollMessages()` entirely and simplify the loop — no message pump, no
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] `GetConsoleWindow`, just the 10ms sleep you already have:
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] while(true){
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 	lineValue = rpcFifo.readLine();
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 	if(lineValue.has_value()){
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 		std::cout << "[RPC] line:" << (lineValue.value()) << std::endl;
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 	}else{
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 		std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(10));
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 	}
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 	if(anyKeyDown()){
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 		std::cout << "[RPC] anykey" << std::endl;
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 	}
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 	if(GetAsyncKeyState(VK_ESCAPE) & 0x8000) break;
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] }
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] The message pump approach (`GetKeyboardState` + `PeekMessage`) is the right tool for GUI
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] applications with a `WndProc`, but for console apps `GetAsyncKeyState` is the way to go.
2026-06-23 09:56:51 [glm-5.2] 
