2026-06-23 11:40:36 [nitro@ryzen5] what is phoff and shoff
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪣 0.7° $0.024 70.95KB 13.08s
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] They're file offsets — byte positions within the ELF file where the two header tables begin.
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] `e_phoff` — Program Header Table Offset
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] - Tells the loader where to find the program headers (segment descriptors).
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] - In `vidbot1.elf` this is `0x34` = 52, which is immediately after the 52-byte ELF header.
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] - Each entry describes a segment — a contiguous chunk of the file that gets mapped into
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] memory at load time. For firmware, that's typically flash (`.text`, `.rodata`) and RAM (`.data`,
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] `.bss`).
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] - The loader reads `e_phnum` entries of `e_phentsize` bytes each, starting at this offset.
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] `e_shoff` — Section Header Table Offset
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] - Tells tools like `readelf`/`elfutil` where to find the section headers.
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] - In `vidbot1.elf` this is `0x3fca8` = 261,032, near the end of the file.
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] - Each entry describes a section — a named region like `.text`, `.data`, `.bss`, `.symtab`,
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] `.shstrtab`.
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] - The section table is primarily for linkers and debuggers, not the runtime loader. Many
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] embedded loaders ignore it entirely.
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] - You read `e_shnum` entries of `e_shentsize` bytes each, starting at this offset.
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] Simple diagram
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] File offset
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] 0x00000 ┌─────────────────────┐
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2]         │  ELF header (52 B)  │
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] 0x00034 ├─────────────────────┤  ← e_phoff
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2]         │  Program headers    │  (8 × e_phentsize)
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2]         │  ...                │
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2]         │  (file data)        │
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] 0x3fca8 ├─────────────────────┤  ← e_shoff
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2]         │  Section headers    │  (32 × e_shentsize)
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2]         └─────────────────────┘
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] So `e_phoff` points at the load map (what goes in memory), and `e_shoff` points at the
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] section map (what's in the file for tooling).
2026-06-23 11:40:50 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:29 [nitro@ryzen5] should i use seek to load or just offsets in memory?
2026-06-23 11:41:44 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪣 0.7° $0.026 72.87KB 14.88s
2026-06-23 11:41:44 [glm-5.2] Good question. Both approaches work — here are the trade-offs:
2026-06-23 11:41:44 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:44 [glm-5.2] Option A: Seek-based (current approach)
2026-06-23 11:41:44 [glm-5.2] Each function opens the file and `seekg` to the offset.
2026-06-23 11:41:44 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:44 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:44 [glm-5.2] std::ifstream file(path, std::ios::binary);
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] file.seekg(hdr.e_phoff);
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] file.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(phdrs.data()), count);
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] • Pros:* Minimal memory — only reads what you need.
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] • Cons:* Opens the file 4+ times (header, phdrs, shdrs, strtab). Each open/seek has small
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] overhead. More error handling.
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] Option B: Load whole file into memory once
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] Read the entire file into a `std::vector<uint8_t>` buffer, then index directly with offsets.
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] std::vector<uint8_t> buf;
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] // read entire file into buf once
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] auto* hdr = reinterpret_cast<struct elf32*>(buf.data());
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] auto* phdrs = reinterpret_cast<struct elf32_phdr*>(buf.data() + hdr->e_phoff);
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] auto* shdrs = reinterpret_cast<struct elf32_shdr*>(buf.data() + hdr->e_shoff);
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] const char* strtab = reinterpret_cast<const char*>(buf.data() + shdrs[hdr->e_shstrndx].sh_offset);
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] • Pros:* One file open, no seeks, pointer math is fast and simple. No repeated I/O. Natural
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] for a small firmware ELF (~260KB).
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] • Cons:* Reads the whole file even if you only want the header. For huge files that's
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] wasteful, but for embedded binaries it's negligible.
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] Recommendation
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] For `vidbot1.elf` at ~260KB, Option B is cleaner — one read, then everything is just
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] pointer arithmetic off the buffer. It also eliminates the repeated `ifstream` opens and makes the
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] code easier to follow.
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] Want me to refactor `elfutil.cpp` to use the buffer approach?
2026-06-23 11:41:45 [glm-5.2] 
