2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] Here are a few things to keep in mind as you move forward with the buffer approach:
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] 1. The Pointer Math Quirk
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] Because your `load_elf32` function reads the 52-byte header into `hdr` and then reads the
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] rest of the file into the `content` vector, your buffer starts at file offset 52.
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] You've already accounted for this perfectly with:
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] `content.data() + (elf32header.e_phoff - sizeof(elf32))`
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] Just remember that any offset you pull from the ELF metadata (like `sh_offset` inside a section
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] header) will also need `sizeof(elf32)` subtracted from it if you want to index it into that
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] `content` buffer.
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] 2. Un-commenting the Structs
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] When you bring `elf32_phdr` and `elf32_shdr` back out of the comment block, you can cast those `ph`
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] and `sh` pointers directly to them.
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] Because your `content` vector holds `char`s, you'll be casting from `const char` to `struct
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] elf32_phdr`.
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] 3. Iterating through headers
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] Once you have the pointer to the first program header, you can step through them. Since
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] `e_phentsize` (and `e_shentsize`) are in the header, you can just add that byte size to your
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] pointer to get to the next entry. In standard ELF32, the entry size is exactly the size of the
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] struct, but using the `entsize` field from the header is the robust way to do it.
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] 4. Enums vs. Maps
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] If you decide to convert the `PT_` and `SHT_` constants to enums like you did with `etype`,
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] remember that `p_type` has some weird values (like `0x6474e551` for GNU_EH_FRAME) and `sh_type` can
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] have target-specific values (like `0x70000001` for ARM attributes). A `std::map<uint32_t,
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] std::string>` is usually safer for those two than a sequential vector, since the values aren't 0,
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] 1, 2, 3...
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:19:03 [glm-5.2] Let me know what part you're working on, or if you want to bounce ideas around as you edit!
