2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] 1. The Buffer Offset Math (`e_shoff - sizeof(elf32)`)
2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] This is the most obvious culprit. Because `load_elf32` reads the 52-byte header into `hdr` and only
2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] puts the rest of the file into `content`, your buffer starts at file offset 52.
2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] To compensate, you have to do `elf32header.e_shoff - sizeof(elf32)`.
2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] While this works for the section table pointer right now, it's going to bite you when you try to
2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] read a section's actual data. If a section header says `sh_offset = 1000`, you'll have to remember
2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] to do `1000 - 52` to find it in your `content` buffer. That's exactly the kind of math that causes
2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] off-by-one bugs.
2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:24:17 [glm-5.2] • The cleaner way: Load the entire* file into `content` (including the ELF header).
2026-06-23 12:24:18 [glm-5.2] Then you can just use `content.data() + elf32header.e_shoff` directly, and any `sh_offset` or
2026-06-23 12:24:18 [glm-5.2] `p_offset` values will work exactly as-is.
