2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] To do this, you need to resolve the `sh_name` field. `sh_name` isn't a string itself; it's just an
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] integer offset into the section header string table.
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] Here is the logic you'll want to follow in `main`:
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] • 1. Grab the string table header*
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] You already know `e_shstrndx` is 31. So you can look at `sh[elf32header.e_shstrndx]` to get the
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] header for the string table section.
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] • 2. Find the string table data in your buffer*
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] That string table header has an `sh_offset` field. This is the file offset where the strings live.
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] • Crucial reminder*: Because your `content` buffer skips the 52-byte ELF header, you'll need to
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] subtract `sizeof(elf32)` from `sh_offset` to get the correct pointer into `content`.
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] So, something like:
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] `const char* strtab = content.data() + (sh[elf32header.e_shstrndx].sh_offset - sizeof(elf32));`
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] • 3. Print the name in your loop*
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] Now `strtab` points to the start of the string table. `sh[i].sh_name` is the index. You can just
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] add them together to print the C-string:
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] `std::cout << "sh" << i << " name: " << (strtab + sh[i].sh_name) << std::endl;`
2026-06-23 12:25:58 [glm-5.2] 
