2026-06-23 12:31:32 [glm-5.2] `e_shstrndx` is 31. It is not a file offset; it is an index into the section header
2026-06-23 12:31:32 [glm-5.2] array. It tells you which of the 32 section headers holds the string table.
2026-06-23 12:31:32 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:31:32 [glm-5.2] To get the actual string data, you need a two-step process:
2026-06-23 12:31:32 [glm-5.2] 1. Use the index to grab the correct section header: `sh[elf32header.e_shstrndx]`.
2026-06-23 12:31:32 [glm-5.2] 2. Look at the `sh_offset` field inside that header to find where the strings live in the
2026-06-23 12:31:33 [glm-5.2] file. Then you apply your `sizeof(elf32)` subtraction to that offset to point into your `content`
2026-06-23 12:31:33 [glm-5.2] buffer.
