2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] │  **p2**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x200001F0`  │  **RAM Data**. Starts in SRAM at `0x200001F0`. This is your `.data` section.                                            │
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] │  **p3**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x100016C0`  │  **Flash Metadata**. Up in Flash, likely `.binary_info`.                                                                │
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] │  **p4**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x20000398`  │  **RAM BSS/Heap**. Higher up in SRAM.                                                                                   │
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] │  **p5**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x20000594`  │  **RAM Scratch/Stack**. Even higher in SRAM.                                                                            │
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] │  **p6**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x20000000`  │  **RAM Vectors**. Starts at the absolute bottom of SRAM (`0x20000000`). This is your `.ram_vector_table`.               │
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] │  **p7**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x20082000`  │  **Stack Pointer!** `0x20082000` is the exact top of the RP2040's SRAM. The stack grows downwards from here.            │
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] ╰───────────┴────────────────┴────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] What this tells us
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] The loader uses these `p_vaddr` (Virtual Address) values to know exactly where to copy things.
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] • It takes `p1`, copies a chunk of the file to `0x10000000` (but since it's execute-in-place flash,
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] it actually just leaves it there).
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] • It takes `p2`, copies initialized variables from Flash to `0x200001F0` in RAM.
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] • It sees `p7` and knows that the stack pointer needs to be initialized to `0x20082000`.
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] You now have a fully functioning memory map parser!
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] If you wanted to take it one step further, printing `p_memsz` and `p_filesz` next to these would
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] show you exactly how many bytes are being copied, and `p_flags` would show you if they are
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] Read-Only (4), Read-Write (6), or Read-Execute (5).
2026-06-23 12:42:14 [glm-5.2] 
