2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] The C++ equivalent is:
2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] #include <cstdint>
2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] The convention in C++ is to drop the `.h` and add a `c` prefix for standard C headers.
2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] The main difference is that `<cstdint>` guarantees the types (like `uint32_t`, `uint8_t`) are
2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] placed in the `std::` namespace (e.g., `std::uint32_t`), though most compilers also make them
2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] available in the global namespace for compatibility.
2026-06-23 12:49:59 [glm-5.2] 
