2026-06-23 13:23:17 [glm-5.2] I noticed a couple of small things in your code that you might want to tweak:
2026-06-23 13:23:17 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:17 [glm-5.2] 1. Potential Crash with `ptypes.find()`
2026-06-23 13:23:17 [glm-5.2] In your `main` loop, you have:
2026-06-23 13:23:17 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] auto ptype=ptypes.find(ph[i].p_type)->second;
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] If `p_type` is a value not in your map (like `0x6474e552`), `.find()` returns `ptypes.end()`.
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] Dereferencing `end()->second` will crash the program.
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] A safer way to do it inline is:
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] auto it = ptypes.find(ph[i].p_type);
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] std::string ptype = (it != ptypes.end()) ? it->second : "UNKNOWN";
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 2. Missing `inline` in `elf32.h`
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] You defined `pflags_string` directly in `elf32.h`:
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] std::string pflags_string(uint32_t flags) { ... }
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] Because it's a standard function (not a template or constexpr), if you ever include `elf32.h` in
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] more than one `.cpp` file, the linker will complain about "multiple definitions". You can fix this
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] by adding the `inline` keyword: `inline std::string pflags_string(...)`.
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 3. Flags aren't printed yet
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] You added the `pflags_string` function, but you haven't actually called it in your `cout` statement
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] for the program headers! You can add it right after `filesz`:
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] << ", filesz:0x" << ph[i].p_filesz
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] << ", flags:" << pflags_string(ph[i].p_flags) // <-- add this
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 13:23:18 [glm-5.2] 
