2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.008 12.90KB 31.73s
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] A hex dump is a natural addition. A few things worth discussing before you start:
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] • Offset model*
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] Your `content` vector deliberately excludes the ELF header — every accessor subtracts
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] `sizeof(elf32)`. That works for structured parsing but a hex dump should show true file offsets,
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] otherwise users can't cross-reference with `objdump` or a hex editor. You have two options:
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] - Dump directly from a re-read of the file (simplest, avoids the offset mismatch entirely).
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] - Keep using `content` but add `sizeof(elf32)` back when printing offsets.
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] The first is cleaner since a hex dump is inherently a raw view.
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] • Scope*
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] What granularity do you want?
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] - Whole file
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] - A section by name or index (using `sh_offset` / `sh_size`)
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] - A segment by index (using `p_offset` / `p_filesz`)
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] - An arbitrary byte range
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] Section-scoped dumps are probably the most useful next step since you already enumerate sections
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] and resolve their names. You could accept a section name as a second argument.
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] • Format*
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] The classic layout is:
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 00000000  7f 45 4c 46 01 01 01 00  00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  |.ELF............|
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] Offset in hex, 16 bytes per line with a gap at the midpoint, then ASCII with non-printables shown
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] as `.`. A column width of 16 is conventional but 8 can be useful for small sections.
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] • One thing to watch*
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] Your `etypes` vector is indexed by `e_type` directly:
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] std::cout << etypes[hdr.e_type]
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] If `e_type` is out of range that will crash. Not related to the hex dump but worth noting since
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] you'll be testing with varied files.
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] Want to start with whole-file dump or section-scoped?
2026-06-23 15:36:22 [glm-5.2] 
