2026-06-23 15:40:00 [nitro@ryzen5] void dumpMem(uint16_t *base,int endmem){	std::stringstream ss;
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.015 14.88KB 41.06s
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] For a classic hex dump, it is generally better to use `uint8_t` (bytes) rather than
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] `uint16_t` (words). Using `uint16_t*` forces 16-bit alignment, which can cause crashes if you
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] pass an odd address on architectures like ARM. It also introduces endianness issues where the bytes
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] might appear swapped compared to the file order. A byte-oriented approach avoids these problems and
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] matches the standard format.
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] Here is a tidy, comment-free implementation using your `stringstream` approach:
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] #include <iostream>
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] #include <iomanip>
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] #include <sstream>
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] #include <cctype>
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] void dumpMem(const uint8_t* base, size_t size) {
2026-06-23 15:40:42 [glm-5.2] 	std::stringstream ss;
