2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] Thank you for sharing the files. I've reviewed all three and have a few observations:
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] Signature mismatch for `dumpMem`
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] In `elf32.h` the declaration is:
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] void dumpMem(const uint8_t* base, size_t size);
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] But in `elfutil.cpp` the forward declaration and call site use a different signature:
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] void dumpMem(const uint8_t* values, int base32, size_t size);
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] This would cause a linker error or undefined behaviour depending on how `dumpMem` is actually
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] defined elsewhere. Worth reconciling.
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] The `sizeof(elf32)` offset convention
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] The approach of reading the ELF header separately into `hdr` and storing only the remainder in
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] `content` means every file offset needs `- sizeof(elf32)`. This works but is fragile — if
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] `sh_offset` or `p_offset` ever points into the header region (e.g. a `SHT_NULL` section at offset
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 0), the subtraction underflows to a very large value. A null check or a `static_cast` to a signed
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] type before subtracting would be safer.
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:20 [glm-5.2] `ptypes.find()` without bounds check
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] In the program header loop:
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] auto ptype=ptypes.find(ph[i].p_type)->second;
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] If `p_type` isn't in the map, `find` returns `end()` and dereferencing it is undefined behaviour.
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] The section header loop has the same pattern with `shtypes.find()`. A guard or a fallback to
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] `"UNKNOWN"` (as done in `emachine_string`) would be more robust.
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] `get_entry_symbol` and `print_symbols`
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] Both cast `content.data() + offset` directly to `elf32_sym*`. On architectures with strict
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] alignment requirements this could fault. Using `memcpy` into a local `elf32_sym` would be portable,
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] though on ARM/x86 it's typically fine in practice.
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] Otherwise
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] The structure definitions in `elf32.h` look correct for ELF32. The lookup tables cover the common
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] types. The relay transcript confirms it successfully parsed an ARM bare-metal executable, which is
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] a good validation. The commented-out `print_symbols` call in `main` suggests that feature is still
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] being tested — let me know if you'd like help debugging or extending it.
2026-06-24 11:17:21 [glm-5.2] 
