2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] Thank you for the update. I see you've introduced a `typeString` function to handle the lookup
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] safely, which is a good step towards reusable code.
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] However, there is a small issue in the implementation in `elf32.h`:
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] std::string typeString(uint32_t ptype){
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] 	if(ptypes.has(ptype)) return ptypes.find(ptype)->second;
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] 	return "[PTYPE_UNKNOWN]";
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] }
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] The `std::map` container does not have a `.has()` method in standard C++. You can use `.count()`
2026-06-24 11:25:31 [glm-5.2] (which returns 1 if the key exists) or, if you are compiling with C++20 support, `.contains()`.
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] Using `.count()` is the most broadly compatible approach:
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] std::string typeString(uint32_t ptype) {
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] 	auto it = ptypes.find(ptype);
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] 	if (it != ptypes.end()) return it->second;
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] 	return "[PTYPE_UNKNOWN]";
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] }
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] Also, while the program header loop in `elfutil.cpp` now uses `typeString`, the section header loop
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] still relies on the unguarded `shtypes.find(sh[i].sh_type)->second`. You might want to create a
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] similar `shtypeString` function or generalize the lookup to cover both maps safely.
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] Let me know if you would like me to provide an example of how to generalize the lookup function.
2026-06-24 11:25:32 [glm-5.2] 
