2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] Thank you for sharing the output. It is great to see the tool running successfully and parsing the
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] ELF file comprehensively.
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] Based on the program headers and section names, this ELF appears to be compiled for a Raspberry Pi
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] Pico (RP2040) or a similar RP2040-based board. A few indicators stand out:
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] • Memory Layout: The program headers show two distinct memory regions. `0x10000000` is the
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] XIP (Execute In Place) flash address, and `0x20000000` is the SRAM base address.
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] • RP2040 Specific Sections: Sections like `.binary_info`, `.ram_vector_table`,
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] `.scratch_x`, and `.scratch_y` are characteristic of the RP2040 C/C++ SDK linker scripts.
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] • Stack and Heap: The presence of `.heap`, `.stack_dummy`, and the SRAM program headers
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] with `filesz:0x0` (indicating NOBITS/uninitialized data) aligns perfectly with the RP2040 memory
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] map, including the stack pointer initialization near the top of SRAM (`0x20081000`).
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] The hex dump of the `.text` section looks correct, displaying what appear to be ARM Thumb-2
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] instructions (e.g., `70 47` being `bx lr`, and `2d e9 ...` being `push` instructions).
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] As a minor note regarding the code from earlier: this specific run avoided the potential
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] `shtypes.find(...)->second` crash because all section types in `vidbot1.elf` exist in your
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] `shtypes` map. However, if you were to parse an ELF with an unknown section type, it would still
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] trigger undefined behavior. Applying the safe lookup pattern mentioned previously will make the
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] tool robust against any ELF file you throw at it.
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] The parser is shaping up nicely. Let me know if you plan to add disassembly capabilities or symbol
2026-06-24 11:28:42 [glm-5.2] resolution next!
