2026-06-24 11:29:33 [nitro@ryzen5] to decode thumb2 what is typical size of op?
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.036 44.99KB 5.28s
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] Thumb-2 is a mixed-length instruction set. Instructions are either 16 bits (2 bytes) or 32 bits (4
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] bytes) long.
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] To determine the size of a specific instruction, a decoder looks at the first 16-bit halfword:
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] • If the first 3 bits are `0b111` (i.e., `0xE800` - `0xFFFF`), it is a 32-bit instruction. The
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] decoder must fetch the next 16-bit halfword to decode the full instruction.
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] • Otherwise, it is a 16-bit instruction.
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] Because of this, decoding Thumb-2 requires reading memory 16 bits at a time and conditionally
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] fetching another 16 bits.
2026-06-24 11:29:39 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:36 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.017 26.24KB 20.46s
2026-06-24 11:44:36 [glm-5.2] Thank you for sharing these files. I've reviewed the full set. Here are some observations:
2026-06-24 11:44:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] • The codebase has clearly evolved since the relay.md transcript.* The suggestions from
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] that session (parse program headers, parse section headers, resolve section names via string table)
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] are all implemented in the current `elfutil.cpp`. The symbol table walk and entry-point symbol
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] lookup are also present, though `get_entry_symbol` is defined but not called in `main`.
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] • One bug worth flagging* in `lmachine.h`, line in `llogic::isbranch()`:
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] inline int isbranch() {
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2]     return (opcode&0x30==0x30);
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] }
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] Due to C++ operator precedence, `==` binds tighter than `&`, so this evaluates as `opcode & (0x30
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] == 0x30)` which is `opcode & 1`. It should be:
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] return ((opcode & 0x30) == 0x30);
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] • Other observations:*
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] - The `content` vector deliberately excludes the 52-byte ELF header, and all subsequent offset
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] calculations subtract `sizeof(elf32)`. This is consistent throughout but fragile if someone adds a
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] new access path without accounting for it.
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] - There's no bounds checking when indexing into `content` via section or program header offsets. A
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] malformed ELF could cause out-of-bounds reads.
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] - `dumpThumb2` and `dumpMem` are declared but their implementations aren't in the shared files —
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] presumably they live in another translation unit.
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] - `thumb2.h` provides a comprehensive set of narrow and wide instruction encoders, but no
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] disassembler is visible. If `dumpThumb2` is meant to decode rather than just hex-dump, that
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] implementation would be interesting to see.
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] - The `rmachine`/`lmachine` hierarchy is an IR-to-native compilation framework with register
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] allocation and basic block analysis — `thumb2` as a code generator for RP2350/Cortex-M33 is a
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] concrete backend for it.
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] Would you like to discuss any particular aspect further, or are you looking to extend the tool in a
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] specific direction?
2026-06-24 11:44:37 [glm-5.2] 
