2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] A strong candidate for testing a Thumb-2 disassembler is a function that computes a 32-bit
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] hash, checksum, or cyclic redundancy check (CRC).
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] Here is why this makes an excellent testbed:
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] 1. Instruction Diversity: Hash and CRC algorithms naturally require a mix of shifts (LSR,
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] LSL, ROR), logical operations (EOR, AND), and arithmetic (ADD, SUB). This ensures your disassembler
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] is exercised across multiple instruction categories rather than just a narrow subset.
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] 2. 32-bit Thumb-2 Encodings: Operations like `ROR` with an immediate, or wide `EOR`
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] operations, often force the compiler to emit 32-bit Thumb-2 instructions rather than the more
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] compact 16-bit Thumb variants. This is crucial for testing the wider decoding paths of your
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] disassembler.
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] 3. Control Flow: If implemented iteratively over an input array, it generates loop
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] structures (comparisons, conditional branches, and pointer arithmetic), allowing you to verify that
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] your disassembler correctly maps branch targets and offsets.
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] 4. Built-in Intrinsics: Compilers often recognize simple CRC or bit-twiddling loops and may
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] substitute them with hardware intrinsics or specialized instructions (like `CLZ` or bitfield
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] insertions), providing interesting edge cases to decode.
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] Alternatively, a Greatest Common Divisor (GCD) function using the Euclidean algorithm is
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] another great numeric function. It relies heavily on conditional logic and modulo arithmetic (which
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] the compiler might implement using `UDIV` or a software division subroutine), generating distinct
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] control flow graphs.
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] To ensure the symbol remains visible in the ELF symbol table for your `print_symbols` and
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] `get_entry_symbol` routines to find:
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] • Avoid marking the function as `static`.
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] • Ensure you are compiling without aggressive stripping (e.g., omit `-s` and consider `-fno-inline`
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] if you want to guarantee the function exists as a discrete block of code rather than being
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] optimized away).
2026-06-24 14:10:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 14:10:49 [nitro@ryzen5] options for basic crc
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.013 14.58KB 39.21s
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] When considering a basic CRC for testing a disassembler, you have a few standard options. The
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] choice between them dictates what kinds of instructions the compiler will emit, so it is worth
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] picking one that covers the architectural behaviors you want to verify.
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] • 1. CRC-32 (IEEE 802.3)*
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] This is the most common 32-bit checksum, used in Ethernet and ZIP files.
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] • Disassembler Coverage: Because it operates on 32-bit words, it heavily exercises bitwise
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] shifts (`LSR`), exclusive ORs (`EOR`), and bit testing (`TST` or `AND`). Processing an input buffer
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] will generate `LDRB` (Load Register Byte) instructions and pointer arithmetic.
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] • Thumb-2 Specifics: The inner loop typically involves shifting a 32-bit register right by
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] 1, testing the lowest bit, and conditionally XORing with a polynomial (like `0xEDB88320`). This
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] generates excellent conditional branch (`BNE`/`BEQ`) sequences.
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] • 2. CRC-16 (CCITT)*
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] This is a 16-bit variant often used in serial communications and embedded systems.
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] • Disassembler Coverage: It generates a similar instruction profile to CRC-32 but might
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] force the compiler to use 16-bit specific instructions or explicit zero-extension instructions
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] (like `UXTH` - Zero Extend Halfword) to keep the upper 16 bits of the 32-bit registers clear. This
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] is highly useful for testing those specific Thumb-2 extension instructions.
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] • 3. Table-Driven CRC vs. Bitwise CRC*
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] How you implement the CRC mathematically changes the output binary significantly:
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] • Bitwise: Calculates the CRC bit-by-bit. This generates tight, predictable loops with
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] logical operations and conditional branches. It is the best choice for testing core ALU operations
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] and branch offset decoding.
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] • Table-Driven: Uses a precomputed 256-entry array to process one byte at a time. This
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] shifts the focus from bitwise logic to memory operations. It will generate `LDR` instructions with
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] computed shifted offsets (e.g., shifting an index left by 2 bytes and adding it to a base address),
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] which tests complex addressing modes but hides the logical bitwise operations behind a memory
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] lookup.
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] 
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] • Recommendation for Testing*
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] A bitwise CRC-32 function that takes a `uint8_t*` data pointer and a `size_t` length is an
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] excellent target. It will yield a public symbol, a prologue/epilogue, stack frame management,
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] pointer iteration, byte loads, and a dense inner loop of bitwise logic and branching.
2026-06-24 14:11:29 [glm-5.2] 
