2026-06-27 14:00:47 [nitro@ryzen5] dumpsegment the PT_LOAD seems like next step i think
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] [gpt-5.3-codex] Agreed — that’s the right next step. 👍
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] `dumpsegment` for each `PT_LOAD` will give you a clean, loader-accurate view of what actually
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] matters at runtime.
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] Best order:
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 1. Dump all `PT_LOAD` entries with:
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - index
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `p_offset`, `p_vaddr`, `p_filesz`, `p_memsz`, flags
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - computed file range `[p_offset, p_offset + p_filesz)`
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 2. For segments with `p_filesz > 0`:
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - hex dump bytes from file (or from your buffer with correct offset mapping)
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 3. For `p_memsz > p_filesz`:
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - report implicit zero-fill size (`p_memsz - p_filesz`) as BSS/reserved tail
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 4. Optional but very useful:
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - annotate symbols that fall inside each segment (`main`, crc funcs, etc.)
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] One caution with your current buffer approach:
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - since `content` starts after ELF header, convert carefully:
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- `content_off = p_offset - sizeof(elf32)`
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - and guard for underflow/overflow before dumping.
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] If you want, I can help you define a compact output format so segment dumps stay readable even for
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] bigger images.
2026-06-27 14:00:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-06-27 14:11:42 [nitro@ryzen5] what is type:ARM_EXIDX
2026-06-27 14:11:46 [gpt-5.3-codex] `ARM_EXIDX` is the ARM exception index table section/segment (`.ARM.exidx`).
2026-06-27 14:11:46 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-06-27 14:11:46 [gpt-5.3-codex] In plain terms:
2026-06-27 14:11:46 [gpt-5.3-codex] - It stores compact unwind metadata for functions.
2026-06-27 14:11:46 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Used by exception unwinding / stack backtrace mechanisms (e.g., C++ exceptions, fault unwinding).
2026-06-27 14:11:46 [gpt-5.3-codex] - It’s part of ARM EABI conventions.
2026-06-27 14:11:46 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-06-27 14:11:46 [gpt-5.3-codex] So in your output, `type:ARM_EXIDX` means that entry contains unwind index data, not executable
2026-06-27 14:11:46 [gpt-5.3-codex] code.
2026-06-27 14:11:46 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
