2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 gemini-3.5-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.024 1.335KB 15.01s
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] It looks like the initial paste suffered from some character encoding issues, but the clean sets of
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] characters you provided in your first example are excellent. Those are standard Unicode
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] Box-Drawing Characters (specifically from the `U+2500` to `U+257F` block).
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] When designing a specification for ASCII or text-based mazes, there are a few established standards
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] and conventions you can follow to make your maze data easy to parse, render, and share.
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 1. The Bitmask Mapping Standard (Recommended)
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] The most robust way to map maze walls to box-drawing characters is using a 4-bit bitmask
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] representing the four cardinal directions: North (Up), East (Right), South (Down), and West
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] (Left).
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] Each direction is assigned a binary value:
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] • North (Up) = 1 (binary `0001`)
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] • East (Right) = 2 (binary `0010`)
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] • South (Down) = 4 (binary `0100`)
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] • West (Left) = 8 (binary `1000`)
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] By adding these values together, every possible wall intersection or path connection results in a
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] unique ID from `0` to `15`. This ID acts as a direct index into a 16-character lookup array.
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] Standard 16-Character Array Mapping:
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] If you expand your 11-character sets to a 16-character array (to include dead-ends and empty
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] space), the index matches the bitmask perfectly:
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] ╭───────────────────┬────────────────────┬─────────────────────┬──────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────╮
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  Index (Decimal)  │  Binary (W-S-E-N)  │  Character (Light)  │  Character (Double)  │  Description                    │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] ├───────────────────┼────────────────────┼─────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  :---             │  :---              │  :---               │  :---                │  :---                           │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **0**            │  `0000`            │  ` ` (Space)        │  ` `                 │  No connections (isolated)      │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **1**            │  `0001`            │  `╵`                │  `╨`                 │  Line pointing North            │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **2**            │  `0010`            │  `╶`                │  `╞`                 │  Line pointing East             │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **3**            │  `0011`            │  `└`                │  `╚`                 │  Corner (North + East)          │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **4**            │  `0100`            │  `╷`                │  `╥`                 │  Line pointing South            │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **5**            │  `0101`            │  `│`                │  `║`                 │  Vertical Line (North + South)  │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **6**            │  `0110`            │  `┌`                │  `╔`                 │  Corner (South + East)          │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **7**            │  `0111`            │  `├`                │  `╠`                 │  Tee (North + East + South)     │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **8**            │  `1000`            │  `╴`                │  `╡`                 │  Line pointing West             │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **9**            │  `1001`            │  `┘`                │  `╝`                 │  Corner (North + West)          │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **10**           │  `1010`            │  `─`                │  `═`                 │  Horizontal Line (East + West)  │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **11**           │  `1011`            │  `┴`                │  `╩`                 │  Tee (North + East + West)      │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **12**           │  `1100`            │  `┐`                │  `╗`                 │  Corner (South + West)          │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **13**           │  `1101`            │  `┤`                │  `╣`                 │  Tee (North + South + West)     │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **14**           │  `1110`            │  `┬`                │  `╦`                 │  Tee (East + South + West)      │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] │  **15**           │  `1111`            │  `┼`                │  `╬`                 │  Cross (All directions)         │
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] ╰───────────────────┴────────────────────┴─────────────────────┴──────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────╯
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] Using this standard, rendering a maze simply requires calculating the neighbors for each wall tile,
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] generating the bitmask value, and pulling the character from your chosen array style.
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:45 [gemini-3.5-flash] 2. The Grid-Cell (Wall vs. Path) Representation
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] In ASCII maze files, there are two common layout specifications:
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] Option A: The "Dense" Grid (Tiles are either Wall or Path)
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] In this format, every character in the text file represents a uniform grid unit. Walls are solid
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] box characters, and paths are spaces.
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] • Pros: Easy to write by hand and render in simple text editors.
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] • Cons: Walls must be at least one character wide, which makes the maze files twice as
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] large as the logical grid.
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] ┌───┬───┐
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] │   │   │
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] │ ┌─┘ ┌─┤
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] │ │   │ │
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] └─┴───┴─┘
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] Option B: The "Coordinate-Free" Line-Based Grid
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] In this format, characters represent only the intersections and walls themselves. The cells (the
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] playable paths) are the empty spaces between the characters.
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] • Pros: Highly compact; represents the mathematical graph perfectly.
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] • Cons: Requires a monospaced font with correct line-height settings to look correct, and
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] cannot easily display a "player" icon inside a cell because the cells do not have their own
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] coordinates in the text file.
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 3. Standard File Formats for Mazes
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] If you are looking to save or share your mazes in a structured format before rendering them to
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] ASCII, two informal standards are widely used:
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 1.  The Daedalus Maze Format: An older but widely recognized format that represents mazes
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] as a grid of hexadecimal numbers, where each number represents the wall state of a cell.
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 2.  JSON Grid representation: A modern approach where the file contains metadata (width,
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] height, style) and a 2D array of integers (using the 4-bit bitmask described above).
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] An example of a clean JSON specification might look like this:
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] {
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 	"width": 5,
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 	"height": 3,
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 	"style": "rounded",
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 	"grid": [
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 		[6, 10, 14, 10, 12],
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 		[5, 6, 9, 3, 13],
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 		[3, 11, 10, 10, 9]
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 	]
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] }
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] If you would like to implement a parser or a rendering function to translate these grid structures
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] into your `boxChars` sets, please let me know and I can provide the helper functions.
2026-06-27 23:00:46 [gemini-3.5-flash] 
