2026-06-28 09:04:51 [nitro@ryzen5] User: gm from aucklandKimi: Good morning from Auckland! Hope you're having a great start to your Sunday. Anything I can help you with today?User: kbot is having a moment on discordKimi: I see - looks like Kimi (the Discord bot) got a bit philosophical and then hit a rate limit. The conversation went from "na tamaki makaurau" (the Maori name for Auckland) to Alaska winters to the "meat bag" caring about latitude, then finally to a practical weather observation about England's heatwave vs. frosty conditions down under.And then... rate limit exceeded. The bot had thoughts but the API said "not so fast."The blocked message is probably the bot trying to finish its thought, or maybe the user sent something that got caught by a filter. Slowmode enabled too - classic Discord moderation move.Is this your server, or did you just catch Kimi having an existential crisis in public?User: I asked K (the bot) and got Error: Rate limit exceeded. Please try again shortly.Kimi: Ah, so you were trying to get a response out of K on Discord and got rate-limited instead. Frustrating when the bot has a moment right when you need it to.The screenshot shows K went full poetic mode - Maori place names, James Cowan, "meat bag" philosophy - then hit the wall on your follow-up about the England heatwave vs. frosty Down Under.A few things that might help:- **Slowmode is on** in that channel (visible at the bottom), so there may be a cooldown between messages- The bot itself might have its own rate limiting per user or per server- If it's a busy server, other users might be hammering the bot tooCould try waiting a minute or two and replying to your own message rather than sending a fresh one - sometimes that bypasses user rate limits if the bot treats threads/replies differently.Or just ask me here instead - no rate limits, and I can handle the weather report without the existential digressions. Unless you want those too.User: the kimi.com free service here does not show tokens used / coal burnt for replies?Kimi: That's right - the kimi.com free service doesn't show token usage or "coal burnt" (energy/cost metrics) for replies. It's a clean, minimal interface without that kind of technical telemetry.Some AI services do expose token counts (like OpenAI's playground or API dashboards), but Kimi's consumer web interface keeps it simple - no usage bars, no cost per message, no carbon footprint calculators.If you're curious about how much you're actually using, there's no built-in meter on the free tier. You'd only see that kind of data if you were on a paid/API plan with usage dashboards, and even then I'm not sure Kimi exposes per-message token counts the way some platforms do.Why the interest - trying to gauge efficiency, or just comparing against what K on Discord might be burning through?User: model 13 under test api versionKimi: I see - you're running your own relay (Relay 1.9.4) that proxies to various AI APIs, and you switched to model #13 which is `kimi-k2.7-code@moonshot`. The test shows it working: `>hi kimi` returned a response in 1.19s with 551B.The price column for Kimi models shows `0.95,0.19,4` - presumably input/output/max pricing tiers or multipliers. But your relay shows `$0.000` for that test call, so either it's a very cheap call or the billing isn't fully wired up yet.You're testing the API version of Kimi rather than the free web interface. The API would expose token counts and costs (unlike kimi.com), but it looks like your relay is abstracting that away with a flat `$0.000` display.Is the test API version behaving differently from what you expected? Or are you checking if the rate limiting you hit on Discord is related to API quotas vs. bot-level throttling?User: lol at abstracting, no that is called fair math rounding, or maybe price undefined will check....Kimi: Fair point - "abstracting" was generous. It's either rounding to zero because the cost was genuinely sub-cent for a tiny 551B call, or the price field is undefined/placeholder for that model and the relay just shows `$0.000` as a default.Looking at the price column again: `0.95,0.19,4` for kimi-k2.7-code. If those are per-million-token rates (input, output, maybe something else), then a 551-byte "hi" call would indeed round to $0.000. The math checks out for fair rounding.But you're right to be suspicious - could also be that the relay hasn't fully wired up Moonshot API billing yet, or the price array parsing is off. The `kimi-k2.6` at `0.95,0.16,4` and `kimi-k2.5` at `0.6,0.1,3` suggest the pricing schema is consistent across the Kimi family.You'll check if it's actually undefined or just microscopically cheap. Either way, the rate limit on Discord is definitely a separate issue from API cost - bot throttling vs. API billing.User: You were the one being suspiciousKimi: Guilty as charged. I was the one side-eyeing the `$0.000` like it might be a placeholder. You were just running your relay and reporting the numbers.Fair math rounding it is - or price undefined, as you said you'll check. My bad for projecting skepticism onto your setup.User: the model all function lists everything at the endpointKimi: Got it - `/model all` dumps the full catalog from the endpoint. That's a lot of models. I see the Gemini section is extensive: image generation (`imagen-4.0`), video generation (`veo-3.1`), embeddings, TTS variants, even `nano-banana-pro` and `antigravity-05-2026`.Most have `count: 0` and no price, which suggests they're discoverable but not necessarily configured or priced in your relay yet. The ones with counts and prices are the actively used ones - `gemini-3.1-pro` (55), `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` (87), `gemini-3.5-flash` (8).The `gemini-robotics-er` at `1,5` is interesting - robotics model with a price, but zero count. And `deep-research` variants with no prices, probably preview/unreleased.Your relay is basically a unified gateway to everything. The endpoint exposes the full menu, but your local config decides what's actually available to switch to.User: it needs an error column, sometimes api support for relay is unsupportedKimi: Yeah, an error column would be useful - flag models where the API support is broken, deprecated, or just not wired up yet in the relay. Something like:```id | model | vendor | count | price | error--|-------|--------|-------|-------|-------12 | kimi-k2.7-code-highspeed | moonshot | 0 | 0.95,0.19,4 | unsupported```Or maybe granular codes: `unsupported`, `rate_limited`, `auth_failed`, `deprecated`, `quota_exceeded`.Right now the zero-count, no-price models are ambiguous - are they just unused, or do they not work? An error column would distinguish "available but nobody's picked it" from "listed but broken."Would you add it as a static config field, or have the relay probe endpoints and populate it dynamically?User: in the box tick column happy to have row of boxes, will add consideration to txtKimi: Got it - in the checkbox column, you're happy with a row of boxes. And you'll add the error column consideration to your todo/notes txt.Pragmatic approach. The checkbox UI pattern works, and documenting the error column idea for later keeps it from getting lost. No need to over-engineer the display right now.User: yes, design says you can have 1 char lolKimi: Hah, one character it is. Design constraints are real - no sprawling error messages in a single-char column. Maybe:- `�` = working- `?` = broken/unsupported- `?` = unknown/untested- `!` = rate limited or degraded- ` ` = blank for unused/irrelevantOr just binary: ` ` vs `?`. One char, one bit of information. Elegant in its constraint.User: and a scan command could double checkKimi: A `/scan` command to double-check - smart. Probe the endpoints in real-time, validate which models are actually responding, and update that single-char status. Could catch drift between config and reality.Something like:```> /scanprobing 213 models...� kimi-k2.7-code ... 1.19s? kimi-k2.7-code-highspeed ... timeout?