2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.003 499B 6.26s
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] If you want the numeric code points:
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] function toCodePoints(input: string): number[] {
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 	const points: number[] = [];
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 	for (const char of input) {
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		const cp = char.codePointAt(0);
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		if (cp !== undefined) {
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 			points.push(cp);
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		}
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 	}
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 	return points;
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] }
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] Usage:
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] toCodePoints("A🚀"); // [65, 128640]
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] If you just want an array of one-character strings for each code point:
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] const chars = Array.from("A🚀"); // ["A", "🚀"]
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] The `for...of` / `Array.from` / spread (`[...str]`) approaches all handle surrogate pairs
2026-06-29 10:23:47 [kimi-k2.7-code] correctly, so characters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane stay as single items.
