2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] No, Microsoft Telnet (the built-in `telnet.exe` on Windows) cannot connect directly to a COM port.
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] It only supports TCP/IP connections (e.g., `telnet host port`).
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] To connect to a serial port, use:
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] - PowerShell (with .NET's `System.IO.Ports.SerialPort`)
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] - PuTTY (select "Serial" connection type)
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] - HyperTerminal (legacy, might not be included in recent Windows)
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Tera Term or MobaXterm
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] Example using PowerShell:
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] $port = new-Object System.IO.Ports.SerialPort "COM3", 9600, None, 8, One
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] $port.Open()
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] $port.WriteLine("AT")
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] $port.Close()
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:50 [nitro@ryzen5] is there a TCP to COM bridge i can design
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 31.46KB 4.02s
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] Yes. You can design a TCP‑to‑COM bridge in two main ways:
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Software bridge – A small TCP server running on your PC that listens on a port and
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] forwards all data to/from a specified COM port.
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Example in Node.js:
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash]    const net = require('net');
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash]    const { SerialPort } = require('serialport');
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash]    const serial = new SerialPort({ path: 'COM3', baudRate: 9600 });
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash]    const server = net.createServer(socket => {
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash]        socket.pipe(serial).pipe(socket);
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash]    });
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash]    server.listen(8080);
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Tools like `socat` also do this:
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash]    socat TCP-LISTEN:8080,reuseaddr,fork FILE:/dev/ttyS0,raw,echo=0
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Hardware bridge – An ESP32 or similar microcontroller running a TCP server
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] (WiFi/Ethernet) and UART to a serial device. Many firmware projects exist (e.g., `esp-link`,
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] `ser2net` for embedded).
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] Which approach fits your use case?
2026-06-29 12:26:57 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:01 [nitro@ryzen5] is there a standard wget for windows?
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 32.59KB 3.65s
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] No, Windows does not include a built-in `wget` command like Linux/macOS.
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] However, you have several alternatives:
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `curl.exe` – Ships with Windows 10 (version 1803+) and Windows 11.
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash]   Example: `curl -O https://example.com/file.zip`
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] - PowerShell – `Invoke-WebRequest` (aliased as `iwr` or `wget` in some contexts, but not
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] the GNU tool).
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash]   Example: `iwr -Uri https://example.com/file.zip -OutFile file.zip`
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Third-party – You can install a standalone `wget` for Windows (e.g., from
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] [eternallybored.org](https://eternallybored.org/misc/wget/) or via `choco install wget`).
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you need the exact GNU `wget` syntax, the third-party option is closest. Otherwise, `curl` or
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] PowerShell work well.
2026-06-29 12:28:05 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:53 [nitro@ryzen5] yes curl is there, can i call curl from deno js in simple example"
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 33.58KB 3.04s
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] Yes, you can call `curl` from Deno using `Deno.Command` (or the older `Deno.run`).
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] • Simple example:*
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] const { stdout } = new Deno.Command("curl", {
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash]   args: ["-s", "https://httpbin.org/get"],
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] }).outputSync();
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] console.log(new TextDecoder().decode(stdout));
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] For async:
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] const { stdout } = await new Deno.Command("curl", {
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash]   args: ["-s", "https://httpbin.org/get"],
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] }).output();
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] console.log(new TextDecoder().decode(stdout));
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] Note: `Deno.Command` requires `--allow-run` permission.
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:28:58 [deepseek-v4-flash] deno run --allow-run script.ts
