2026-06-29 12:29:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] To expose a basic HTTP GET endpoint from your JSON‑RPC relay (assuming you're using Deno's built‑in
2026-06-29 12:29:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] HTTP server), you can add a route that responds to `GET` requests alongside your POST‑only JSON‑RPC
2026-06-29 12:29:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] handler.
2026-06-29 12:29:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:29:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] • Example (Deno with `std/http`):*
2026-06-29 12:29:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:29:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:29:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] import { serve } from "https://deno.land/std@0.208.0/http/server.ts";
2026-06-29 12:29:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:29:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] async function handler(req: Request): Promise<Response> {
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]   const url = new URL(req.url);
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]   // Simple GET endpoint: return "OK"
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]   if (req.method === "GET" && url.pathname === "/status") {
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]     return new Response("OK", { status: 200 });
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]   }
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]   // JSON‑RPC POST handler (example)
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]   if (req.method === "POST" && url.pathname === "/rpc") {
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]     const body = await req.json();
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]     // ... your JSON‑RPC logic here ...
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]     return new Response(JSON.stringify({ result: "pong" }), {
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]       headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]     });
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]   }
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash]   return new Response("Not Found", { status: 404 });
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] serve(handler, { port: 8080 });
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] You can expand the GET handler to return any data (e.g., relay status, health metrics) by
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] constructing the response body accordingly.
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you want the GET to be part of the JSON‑RPC protocol itself (a method like `"get"`), just handle
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] it inside your existing RPC method dispatch. But "expose a basic get" usually means an HTTP GET
2026-06-29 12:29:43 [deepseek-v4-flash] endpoint.
