2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] After reviewing your `maze.ts` code and the output in `notes.txt`, I can see you're building a maze
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] visualization pipeline:
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Define a plain text maze (`plain`) using `#` (walls) and spaces.
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Double each cell (using `doubleLines`) to create a coarse grid.
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3. Convert that to a block grid (`blockHashGrid`) where walls become `▣` and open spaces become
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] `▢`.
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] 4. Apply a second pass (`outlineGrid`) to replace `▢` cells adjacent to walls with Unicode
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] box‑drawing characters, intending to produce clean corridor outlines.
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] The output shows some box‑drawing characters appear (e.g., `╭`, `╰`, `┤`, `├`), but most interior
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] cells remain as `▢`, and the overall outer border uses rounded corners while inner walls are
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] incomplete. This suggests the algorithm for deciding which box character to use isn't working as
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] intended.
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] • Key observations about the current logic:*
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] - In `outlineGrid`, the condition is
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash]   if (char == "▢" && a9.includes(9635)) { // 9635 is '▣'
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:44:25 [deepseek-v4-flash]   It only attempts to replace `▢` cells that have at least one wall (`▣`) in their 3×3
2026-06-29 13:44:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] neighbourhood. That part is sensible.
2026-06-29 13:44:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:44:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] - However, the `udlr` function (called to determine the exact `Edge` index) checks for codepoint
2026-06-29 13:44:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] `9634` (`▢`) in the four cardinal positions:
2026-06-29 13:44:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:44:26 [deepseek-v4-flash]   let u = a9[1] == 9634;
2026-06-29 13:44:26 [deepseek-v4-flash]   let d = a9[7] == 9634;
2026-06-29 13:44:26 [deepseek-v4-flash]   let l = a9[3] == 9634;
2026-06-29 13:44:26 [deepseek-v4-flash]   let r = a9[5] == 9634;
