2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] Good progress with inverting the neighbor check – now `udlr` looks for walls (`▣`) rather than open
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] cells. However, the resulting output still shows many `*` (fallback) and unexpected characters like
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] `╯` and `╰` on the borders. The root cause is that `clampSample33` returns `0` for out‑of‑bounds
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] cells, and `0 != 9634` evaluates to `true`, so the algorithm treats the edge of the grid as a wall.
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] That causes false connections and incorrect edge types.
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] • Fix:* In `udlr`, treat `0` (or any value that is not a wall `▣`) as “no wall”:
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] function udlr(a9: number[]): number {
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash]     const u = a9[1] === 9635;  // '▣'
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash]     const d = a9[7] === 9635;
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash]     const l = a9[3] === 9635;
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash]     const r = a9[5] === 9635;
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash]     if (u || d || l || r) {
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash]         return getEdge(u, d, l, r);
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash]     } else {
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash]         return -1;
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash]     }
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] This makes the algorithm only react to actual walls, not to out‑of‑bounds or open cells.
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] • Additional adjustments:*
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The outer border of `▢` cells produced by `blockHashGrid` will now correctly remain as `▢` (no
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] walls in the neighbourhood), which is probably fine. If you want a nice frame, you can add a
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] separate pass to draw the outer box.
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] - After the fix, open cells that are adjacent to walls will be replaced with the correct
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] box‑drawing characters, producing a clean outline of the solid wall areas.
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The `*` fallback will rarely appear – only when a wall neighbour exists but the pattern doesn’t
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] match any edge (which shouldn’t happen if the grid is correct).
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you still get artifacts, consider processing `▣` cells as well (to turn the solid wall blocks
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] into continuous lines). That would involve a second pass where wall cells are replaced by box
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] characters based on their own neighbourhood. But for a pure “outline of solid areas”, the current
2026-06-29 13:48:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] approach (only converting open cells adjacent to walls) should suffice after the fix above.
