2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Bit‑extraction logic is incorrect for quad indices
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    The `quads` array maps a 4‑bit pattern (one bit per corner of a 2×2 block) to a Unicode
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] character. Your current code extracts two bits per cell (`bit01`, `bit23`) and combines
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] them with a shift. Instead, you need to extract a single bit from each of the four cells, then
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] assemble them into a nibble:
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    - Top‑left: `(grid[offset] >> shift) & 1`
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    - Top‑right: `(grid[offset] >> (shift+1)) & 1`
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    - Bottom‑left: `(grid[offset+span] >> shift) & 1`
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    - Bottom‑right: `(grid[offset+span] >> (shift+1)) & 1`
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    - Index = `(tl << 3) | (tr << 2) | (bl << 1) | br`
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Your current use of `>>2` for offset and `&3<<shift` extracts two bits at once, but then the
2026-06-30 16:44:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] index calculation would treat them as a pair of 2‑bit values, not a 4‑bit quad pattern.
