2026-06-30 16:57:25 [nitro@ryzen5] for wasm is uint8array for sharing memory?
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.009 102.2KB 8.14s
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] Yes, `Uint8Array` is commonly used to access WebAssembly linear memory from JavaScript.
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] - WebAssembly linear memory is a contiguous block of bytes, exposed as a `WebAssembly.Memory`
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] object.
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] - That memory object has a `.buffer` property which is an `ArrayBuffer` (or `SharedArrayBuffer` for
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] shared memory between threads).
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] - You create a `Uint8Array` view over that buffer to read and write bytes directly.
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] For example:
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] const memory = new WebAssembly.Memory({ initial: 1 });  // 64 KB
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] const bytes = new Uint8Array(memory.buffer);
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] bytes[0] = 42;   // write to Wasm memory
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you need shared memory (e.g., between a main thread and a worker, or between multiple
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] Wasm instances), you use `SharedArrayBuffer` and pass it to `WebAssembly.Memory` with `shared:
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] true`. Then you can create `Uint8Array` views on that shared buffer from multiple contexts – but
2026-06-30 16:57:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] you must use `Atomics` for safe concurrent access.
