2026-06-30 18:23:17 [nitro@ryzen5] what is it for home the cursor to top left
2026-06-30 18:23:18 [nitro@ryzen5] let cursorHome="\x1b[K";
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.013 146.0KB 4.51s
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] The escape sequence `\x1b[K` you wrote is not for homing the cursor – it's for erasing
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] from the cursor position to the end of the line (Clear Line, suffix `K`).
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] To move the cursor to the home position (top‑left corner, row 1, column 1), you need:
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] \x1b[H
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] or equivalently:
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] \x1b[1;1H
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `\x1b` is the ESC character.
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `[H` is the Cursor Position command (also called CUP) with no arguments, which defaults to row 1,
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] column 1.
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you want to store it in a variable:
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] const cursorHome = "\x1b[H";
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you also want to clear the entire screen and home the cursor, use `\x1b[2J\x1b[H` (clear entire
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] screen, then home).
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] So, in your code, replace:
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] let cursorHome = "\x1b[K";   // wrong – this erases line
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] with:
2026-06-30 18:23:34 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:35 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:35 [deepseek-v4-flash] let cursorHome = "\x1b[H";   // moves cursor to top-left
2026-06-30 18:23:35 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 18:23:35 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-01 07:08:50 [nitro@ryzen5] types of books about peoples lives
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 493B 4.93s
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] There are several recognized types of books focusing on people's lives, each distinguished by
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] perspective, scope, and narrative style.
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Autobiography – A first‑person account written by the subject themselves, covering their
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] entire life or major portions of it.
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Memoir – Also first‑person but focuses on a specific period, theme, or set of experiences
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] rather than the whole life.
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Biography – A third‑person account written by someone else, aiming to present a factual,
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] researched portrait of the subject.
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Hagiography – A biography that deliberately idealises the subject, often used for saints
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] or revered figures.
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Pathography – A biography that emphasises the subject’s flaws, struggles, or illnesses,
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] sometimes seen as a critical reaction to hagiography.
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Oral history – A collection of personal recollections, often transcribed from interviews,
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] capturing the lived experience of individuals (sometimes multiple narrators).
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Collected letters or diaries – Published primary sources that reveal a person’s life
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] through their own correspondence or daily entries.
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Biographical novel – A work of fiction that uses imaginative licence to dramatise and
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] interpret a real person’s life.
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Travel memoir – A memoir structured around journeys; the person’s life is revealed
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] through places and encounters.
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-01 07:08:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] These categories often overlap (e.g., a memoir can be part autobiography) but each has a distinct
2026-07-01 07:08:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] emphasis on authorship, scope, and intent.
2026-07-01 07:08:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
