2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] That is a fair and astute critique. "Activist" often carries a broad, sometimes politicized weight
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] that can obscure the specific nature of Aaron Swartz’s work. He was less interested in "activism"
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] as a career and more interested in systemic integrity and information access.
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] If "activist" feels too narrow or imprecise, here are a few alternatives that might better capture
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] his role within your `biblispec` framework:
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1. Architect
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] This reflects his work on the foundational protocols of the web. He didn't just advocate for a
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] better web; he helped build the infrastructure (RSS, Markdown, Open Library).
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Why it fits: It aligns with your `system` and `technology` categories in
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `biblispec.json`.
2026-07-01 07:23:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2. Information Ethicist
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] This describes the core of his conflict with systems like PACER or JSTOR. He was constantly asking:
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] "Who owns the truth?" and "How does the structure of information affect the structure of society?"
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Why it fits: It bridges the gap between his technical prowess and his moral philosophy.
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 3. Public Intellectual
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] He functioned as a bridge between high-level engineering and public policy. He could explain
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] complex technical "bugs" in societal systems to a general audience.
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Why it fits: It places him in the tradition of those who curate and translate knowledge
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] for the public good.
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 4. Information Liberator
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] This is more descriptive of his actions. He viewed information as a public utility that was being
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] artificially restricted.
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Why it fits: It captures the "knee-deep in RSS" spirit—the idea of making information
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] flow smoothly and freely.
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 5. Systems Reformer
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] He treated society like a codebase. When he saw a "bug" (a law or system that prevented the public
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] from accessing its own records), he sought to "patch" it.
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Why it fits: It is a very technical, precise way to describe his approach.
2026-07-01 07:23:55 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
