2026-07-01 08:48:17 [nitro@ryzen5] how can i listen for clicks
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 gemini-3.1-flash-lite 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.015 153.4KB 3.53s
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] To listen for mouse clicks in a Deno terminal application, you must enable Mouse Tracking
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] via ANSI escape sequences.
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Standard `Deno.stdin.setRaw(true)` only captures keyboard events. To capture mouse inputs, you need
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] to tell the terminal to send mouse coordinates as part of the input stream.
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1. Enable Mouse Tracking
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Send this escape sequence to `stdout` at the start of your program:
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `\x1b[?1000h` (Basic tracking) or `\x1b[?1006h` (SGR mode, which is much easier to parse).
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2. Update your `keyboardTask`
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] When mouse tracking is enabled, the terminal sends a specific sequence when you click:
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `\x1b[<B;X;YM`.
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • B: Button code (0=Left, 1=Middle, 2=Right).
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • X, Y: Coordinates (1-based).
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:21 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Here is the minimal logic to integrate into your existing `keyboardTask`:
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] // Enable mouse tracking (SGR mode)
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Deno.stdout.write(new TextEncoder().encode("\x1b[?1006h"));
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] export async function keyboardTask() {
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     Deno.stdin.setRaw(true);
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     const buffer = new Uint8Array(1024);
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     while (running) {
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]         const n = await Deno.stdin.read(buffer);
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]         if (!n) break;
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]         const input = new TextDecoder().decode(buffer.subarray(0, n));
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]         // Detect SGR mouse sequence: starts with \x1b[<
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]         if (input.startsWith("\x1b[<")) {
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]             const parts = input.slice(3, -1).split(';');
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]             const button = parseInt(parts[0]);
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]             const x = parseInt(parts[1]);
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]             const y = parseInt(parts[2]);
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]             console.log(`Clicked at ${x},${y} with button ${button}`);
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]         }
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     }
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] }
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 3. Cleanup
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] When the program exits, you must disable mouse tracking, otherwise the user's terminal will
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] remain in a "weird" state where clicking selects text incorrectly. Send this before
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `stopRunning()`:
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `Deno.stdout.write(new TextEncoder().encode("\x1b[?1006l"));`
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Implementation for `grid.ts`
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Since your `grid.ts` uses a `Bitmap` windowing system, you can now map those `x, y` coordinates
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] directly to your `gridWindow` parameters:
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1.  Capture the click `x, y`.
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2.  Adjust for the terminal's font aspect ratio (usually 2:1 height-to-width).
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 3.  Add `cursorX` and `cursorY` offsets to see which "book" or "spec" was clicked.
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Warning:* Not all terminal emulators support SGR mouse mode equally (though VS Code,
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] iTerm2, and modern Linux terminals do). Test it in your specific environment first.
2026-07-01 08:48:22 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
