2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] In JavaScript/TypeScript, `subarray` does not exist on `string[]` (Arrays).
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `subarray` is a method that belongs to TypedArrays (like `Uint8Array`, `Int32Array`, etc.)
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] and `Buffer`.
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1. If you have an `Array` (e.g., `string[]`)
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] If you want a part of a standard array, you must use `.slice()`.
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const myArray: string[] = ["a", "b", "c", "d"];
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const part = myArray.slice(1, 3); // ["b", "c"]
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • `.slice(start, end)`: Returns a new array containing elements from `start` up to (but not
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] including) `end`.
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • The original array remains unchanged.
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2. If you have a `TypedArray` (e.g., `Uint8Array`)
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] This is where `subarray` is used. You are likely seeing it in your `terminal.ts` because
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `Deno.stdin.read` returns data into a `Uint8Array`.
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const myBuffer = new Uint8Array([10, 20, 30, 40]);
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const part = myBuffer.subarray(1, 3); // [20, 30]
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • `.subarray(start, end)`: Returns a new view on the same memory as the original
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] array.
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Crucial Difference: If you modify `part` (e.g., `part[0] = 99`), it will change
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] the original `myBuffer`.
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Summary for your code:
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • `terminal.ts`: You use `keyboardBuffer.subarray(0, bytesRead)`. This is correct because
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `keyboardBuffer` is a `Uint8Array`. It efficiently points to the slice of the buffer containing the
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] new data without copying it.
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Anywhere else: If you are dealing with a list of strings (like `lines: string[]`), always
2026-07-01 09:02:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] use `.slice()`.
