2026-07-01 09:04:48 [nitro@ryzen5] is there a flush for this
2026-07-01 09:04:49 [nitro@ryzen5] 	Deno.stdout.write(encoder.encode(mouseOn));
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 gemini-3.1-flash-lite 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.016 162.4KB 3.91s
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] In Deno (and Node.js), `Deno.stdout.write()` is already synchronous and unbuffered when
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] writing to a terminal or a pipe. There is no explicit `flush()` method needed because the data is
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] sent to the OS immediately.
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] However, there is a common pitfall: Buffering at the Terminal Emulator level.
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Why it might seem like it needs a flush:
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] If you are writing many small sequences (like mouse codes and cursor movements) in a very tight
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] loop, the terminal emulator (the window you are looking at) might be delaying the visual update to
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] save CPU.
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] If you feel it's lagging:
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1.  Use `Deno.stdout.sync()`: If you want to ensure the OS has physically handed the bytes
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] to the terminal, you can call `Deno.stdout.sync()`. This forces a flush of any kernel-level
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] buffers.
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     Deno.stdout.writeSync(encoder.encode(mouseOn));
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     Deno.stdout.sync();
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2.  Use `writeSync` instead of `write`: Since `write` returns a Promise (making it
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] asynchronous), it can occasionally cause interleaving if you have multiple parts of your code
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] trying to write to the terminal simultaneously. In a terminal UI loop, `Deno.stdout.writeSync` is
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] often more reliable and prevents "tearing" or lag.
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] A note on your loop
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] In your `grid.ts` loop, you are doing:
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] console.log(cursorHome);
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] console.log(gridTitle);
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] console.log(blocks.join("\n"));
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `console.log` itself is a wrapper around `Deno.stdout.writeSync` with a newline added. It is
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] inherently "flushed."
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Recommendation: If the mouse tracking isn't responding, it is almost certainly because
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] the terminal needs the "Private Mode" set. Ensure you are sending the entire*
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] sequence:
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const mouseOn = "\x1b[?1000h\x1b[?1006h"; // 1000h for basic, 1006h for SGR
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Deno.stdout.writeSync(encoder.encode(mouseOn));
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Does the mouse cursor change or the terminal show any reaction when you click after sending
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] that command?* (Some terminals, like standard Windows Command Prompt, simply do not support
2026-07-01 09:04:54 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] mouse reporting regardless of the code).
