2026-07-01 09:27:48 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] To get the width of the console in Deno, you use `Deno.consoleSize()`.
2026-07-01 09:27:48 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:48 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] The Code
2026-07-01 09:27:48 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:48 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const { columns, rows } = Deno.consoleSize();
2026-07-01 09:27:48 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] console.log(`The terminal is ${columns} columns wide and ${rows} rows high.`);
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Important Details:
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1.  Sync vs. Async: `Deno.consoleSize()` is synchronous and returns the dimensions of the
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] file descriptor associated with `stdout`.
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2.  Handling Resize: If the user resizes their terminal window while your `grid.ts` loop is
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] running, the values from a single call at the start will become stale.
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     *   If you need a responsive UI, call `Deno.consoleSize()` inside your `while(isRunning())`
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] loop before you render your `blocks`.
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 3.  Error Handling: If you redirect the output of your program to a file (e.g., `deno run
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] grid.ts > log.txt`), `Deno.consoleSize()` will throw an error (because a file doesn't have a
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] "size"). Always wrap it in a `try/catch` or provide defaults:
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] function getTerminalDimensions() {
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     try {
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]         return Deno.consoleSize();
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     } catch {
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]         return { columns: 80, rows: 24 }; // Fallback
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     }
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] }
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Integration Tip for `grid.ts`:*
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Since you are using a fixed `vidWidth=72` and `vidHeight=16`, you can use `Deno.consoleSize()` to
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] center your grid dynamically:
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const { columns } = getTerminalDimensions();
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const padding = Math.floor((columns - vidWidth) / 2);
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] // Use 'padding' to print spaces before your grid lines
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Does your terminal GUI need to adapt to the window size, or are you sticking to your fixed `72x16`
2026-07-01 09:27:49 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] viewport?
