2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] The architectural shift in `grid.ts`—moving toward a `mainMenu` toggle and a `menuWall`—is a
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] logical step for a terminal-based UI. You are essentially building a compositor that can
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] swap between a "viewport" and a "navigational state."
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Observations on the current implementation:
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1.  The "Status" Buffer: You are using `status.slice(-3)` to show the most recent
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] keyboard/mouse events. This is a very effective way to debug the raw input stream (especially once
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] you start parsing those mouse sequences).
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2.  Dynamic Viewport: By dynamically updating `vidWidth` based on `Deno.consoleSize()`,
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] you’ve moved from a hardcoded UI to a responsive one. This is a significant improvement in
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] usability.
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 3.  Menu Integration: The `menuWall` function is a simple but effective way to shift your
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] coordinate system to make room for a side-bar menu without rewriting the `gridWindow` logic.
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Suggestions for the "Menu" logic:
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Since you have an `apps` array and are using an `index` variable, you can make the menu interactive
2026-07-01 09:41:17 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] by mapping the `y` coordinate of a mouse click to the `apps` array index.
