2026-07-01 09:47:26 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] There are three common ways to convert a string to an integer in JavaScript/TypeScript.
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1. `parseInt()` (The Standard)
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] This is the most common method. It parses a string and returns an integer. It stops parsing as soon
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] as it hits a non-numeric character.
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const num = parseInt("123");     // 123
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const num2 = parseInt("123px");  // 123 (stops at 'p')
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2. `Number()` (The Strict Way)
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] This converts the entire string to a number. If the string contains any non-numeric characters, it
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] returns `NaN` (Not a Number).
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const num = Number("123");       // 123
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const num2 = Number("123px");    // NaN
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 3. The Unary Plus `+` (The Concise Way)
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] This is a shorthand way to cast a string to a number. It is very fast and commonly used in modern
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] JS code.
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const num = +"123";              // 123
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Which one to use for your `menu.ts`?
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Since you are dealing with user input (which might have spaces or unexpected characters),
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `parseInt()` is the safest choice for your menu.
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Best practice for your menu:*
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 09:47:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const choice = prompt("Select an app:");
