2026-07-01 12:46:14 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Key points to remember:
2026-07-01 12:46:14 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • File Extensions: Unlike Node.js, Deno requires the explicit file extension (`.ts`, `.js`,
2026-07-01 12:46:14 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `.tsx`, etc.) in import statements.
2026-07-01 12:46:14 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Relative Paths: Always prefix local files with `./` or `../`.
2026-07-01 12:46:14 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Named Exports: If you have multiple classes in one file, you can export them individually
2026-07-01 12:46:14 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] and import them using curly braces: `import { ClassA, ClassB } from "./models.ts";`.
2026-07-01 12:46:14 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Default Exports: You can also use `export default class User { ... }`, which allows you
2026-07-01 12:46:14 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] to import it without braces: `import User from "./User.ts";`.
2026-07-01 12:46:14 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:49:21 [nitro@ryzen5] deno command to position cursor
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]  ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 gemini-3.1-flash-lite 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.000 2.167KB 2.56s
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Deno does not have a built-in command to control the terminal cursor position. Deno is a runtime
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] for executing JavaScript/TypeScript, and it does not inherently manage terminal UI state.
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] To manipulate the cursor position in a terminal using Deno, you typically use ANSI escape
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] codes. These are special character sequences that terminals interpret as commands.
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Here is a reusable function to move the cursor:
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] /**
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]  * Moves the terminal cursor to a specific row and column.
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]  * Rows and columns are 1-indexed.
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]  */
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] function setCursorPosition(row: number, col: number): void {
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	const escapeCode = `\x1b[${row};${col}H`;
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	Deno.stdout.writeSync(new TextEncoder().encode(escapeCode));
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] }
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] // Example usage:
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] setCursorPosition(5, 10);
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] console.log("Hello at row 5, col 10");
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Explanation of the code:
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • `\x1b[`: This is the Control Sequence Introducer (CSI).
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • `row;colH`: The `H` command tells the terminal to move the cursor to the specified
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] coordinates.
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • `Deno.stdout.writeSync`: This writes the raw bytes directly to the standard output, which
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] the terminal emulator then processes.
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Common Cursor ANSI Codes:
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Hide Cursor: `\x1b[?25l`
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Show Cursor: `\x1b[?25h`
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Clear Screen: `\x1b[2J`
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Move Up N lines: `\x1b[NA`
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] If you are building a complex terminal application (like a TUI or an interactive CLI), you might
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] consider using a library like [dnt](https://github.com/denoland/dnt) or looking for Deno-compatible
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] terminal modules on [jsr.io](https://jsr.io/) (such as `jsr:@cliffy/ansi`), which provide
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] abstraction layers over these escape codes.
2026-07-01 12:49:25 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
