2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Yes, you can enable or disable the blinking effect of the cursor using specific ANSI escape codes.
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Similar to moving the cursor, you send these sequences to `stdout`. Note that support for these
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] codes depends on the terminal emulator you are using (e.g., VS Code terminal, iTerm2, Windows
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Terminal, etc.), as some terminals may ignore them.
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Here are the reusable functions to control the blink state:
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] /**
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]  * Enables the blinking cursor.
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]  */
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] function enableCursorBlink(): void {
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	const escapeCode = "\x1b[?12h";
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	Deno.stdout.writeSync(new TextEncoder().encode(escapeCode));
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] }
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] /**
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]  * Disables the blinking cursor (makes it steady).
2026-07-01 12:53:35 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]  */
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] function disableCursorBlink(): void {
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	const escapeCode = "\x1b[?12l";
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	Deno.stdout.writeSync(new TextEncoder().encode(escapeCode));
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] }
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] // Example usage:
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] disableCursorBlink();
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] console.log("The cursor is now steady.");
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Explanation of the codes:
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • `\x1b[?12h`: The `h` (high/set) suffix enables the "cursor blink" mode.
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • `\x1b[?12l`: The `l` (low/reset) suffix disables the "cursor blink" mode.
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Important Considerations:
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1.  Terminal Compatibility: While these are standard DEC private mode sequences, some
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] lightweight or older terminal emulators may not support them.
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2.  Cleanup: If you disable the blink in your program, it is generally good practice to
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] re-enable it (or reset the terminal state) before your program exits, so the user's terminal
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] returns to its expected behavior.
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 3.  Visibility: These codes only affect the blinking behavior. If you want to hide
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] the cursor entirely, you would use `\x1b[?25l` to hide it and `\x1b[?25h` to show it again.
2026-07-01 12:53:36 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
